// Runtime/SchedRuntime.maxon — ⭐⭐ **THE PROCESSOR (P) AND THE WORKER M — the GMP scheduler's structure,
// built and DELIBERATELY LEFT AT ONE M (G1).**
//
// `GtRuntime.maxon` beside this file owns the GREEN THREAD (G): its struct, its stack, its run queue, its
// timers. This file owns the two things G alone cannot express — **which OS thread is running it (M)** and
// **the per-processor state that thread owns while it does (P)**. Go's model exactly, and both references'
// (`GtLayout.cs:360-402` for the layout, `X64Backend.maxon:8990-9340` for the Win32 mechanics).
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE INVARIANT THIS FILE EXISTS TO STATE, AND IT IS WRITTEN DOWN HERE ONCE (EC10).**
//
// > **A COROUTINE — what this runtime calls a GT that `async` created — is owned by one green thread and is
// > driven ONLY by that green thread's chain of drivers; it is published only to its owner's coroutine queue
// > and never to a P ring or the global queue. A green thread runs on one M at a time. Therefore every
// > refcount read-modify-write on a box happens on the M running that box's one owning green thread, and
// > refcounts are plain (`MmRuntime.emitAdjustRefcount`). The cross-thread writers that remain — the IOCP
// > completion loop re-readying a parked coroutine onto its owner's queue, and (once `spawn` exists) worker
// > Ms publishing, popping and stealing GREEN THREADS — touch queues, status words, counters and claims,
// > every one of which states its `MultiMSharing` class; none touches a refcount.**
//
// ⭐ **THE LAST CLAUSE IS MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED, AND IT HAD TO BE — the plain refcount rests on it.** An
// emitted program creates an OS thread at exactly two `osThreadCreate` sites: `buildIoInit`'s completion
// thread and `emitSchedWakeOrSpawn`'s worker M, which since EC10 has no caller with a producer. So there is
// ONE non-owning OS thread today, and its whole Maxon call graph — read off the emitted bytes of a program
// using `async`, a subprocess and a heap `String` — is `__io_completion_loop` → `__gt_coro_enqueue`: one
// direct call, five Win32 IAT calls, no further Maxon function, and not one `lock` prefix. A queue append
// is the entire job. ⚠ **A rung that gives that thread more to do owes this measurement again** — the
// refcount door's argument is exactly this graph.
//
// ⚖ **USER, 2026-08-27:** *"async is not supposed to create a new green thread. it allows a function to yield
// the current green thread while waiting for a blocking operation"*, and *"the language is supposed to
// guarantee that only 1 green thread can hold references to a box at a time"*. Crossing threads is actors,
// which is not built. `GtRuntime.GtOffOwner` is where the ownership is a FIELD rather than a convention.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE PROCESS RUNS ON ONE M, AND SINCE EC10 THAT IS A CONSEQUENCE RATHER THAN A POLICY.**
// `DefaultMaxProcs` is **1**, so `__sched_init_procs` builds exactly one P — but raising `MAXON_MAX_PROCS`
// no longer starts a worker either, because the only thing that starts one is `__sched_wake_or_spawn` and
// **nothing reaches it**: it is called from the green-thread arms of the publish doors and from the wake a
// locked publish owes, and only a GREEN THREAD selects any of those — which nothing creates until `spawn`. G1 built the whole P/M substrate — the CAS claim, the
// Dekker park, the wake, the per-P system stack — and W212 built the run-queue hierarchy on top of it;
// EC10 leaves both intact and UNREACHED, which is a stronger statement than *"turned off by a constant"*
// and is what makes the refcount argument above hold at every `MAXON_MAX_PROCS`.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY UNREACHED PIECE SAYS SO AT ITS OWN DECLARATION AND NAMES `spawn`**, which is this file's
// existing idiom for *"built, waiting for its producer"* (`POffRunnext`, `POffPendingWaiter`, the P
// free-list slots). G2's audit below is unchanged and still discharged: it is what will still be true the
// day `spawn` gives the tier its producer back.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE AUDIT IS NO LONGER PROSE — IT IS `MultiMSharing`, A REQUIRED ARGUMENT ON EVERY RUNTIME `.data`
// SLOT'S BIRTH (`pushRuntimeGlobal`, below).** This paragraph used to BE the audit: a list derived by grepping
// the tree for *"safe because single-M"*, which is what found its last four items — and exactly the property
// a list in a comment cannot keep, because nothing makes the next slot join it. Every slot the six producers
// in this runtime lay out now states its own class where it is created, and a slot whose author has not
// decided does not compile. The classes and what each promises are on the enum.
//
// ⚠⚠ **WHAT G2 FLIPPED, AND WHAT IT LEFT — THE SHORT FORM. The long form is at each slot.**
//
//   1. **THE REFCOUNT READ-MODIFY-WRITE — G2 MADE IT ATOMIC AND ⭐ EC10 TOOK THE PREFIX BACK OFF**, because
//      the invariant at the top of this file removed the second party rather than serialising against it.
//      What SURVIVES from G2 is the half that was load-bearing: `emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner` DECREMENTS
//      FIRST and picks its arm from the value the decrement displaced — *the order, not the prefix, is what
//      makes it correct*, which G2's own note said and EC10 is the experiment for. `MmRuntime.
//      emitAdjustRefcount` carries the argument and the measurement. ⚠ G2 cited its reproducer as
//      `temp/g2/rc-field.maxon`; `temp/` is gitignored and swept, so that file no longer exists and the
//      measurement is now `maxon-shv2/track0/refcount-torture.maxon`, COMMITTED, driven by
//      `track0/refcount-race.sh`.
//   2. **EVERY `.data` COUNTER**, through the one `MmRuntime.emitGlobalAccumulate`, which is what the four
//      scheduler counters (`__gt_live_count`, `__gt_trace_counter`, `__io_pending`, `__io_pipe_seq`) call
//      instead of each spelling load/add/store by hand. ⚠ Since EC8 that door emits an `atomicRmw` only
//      where a second OS thread can exist (`multiM`, which is `usage.usesGt`) — the four above are built
//      only under `usesGt`, so THEY are always atomic; what stopped being atomic is the box layer's
//      counters in a program that has one thread, where there is no second writer to serialise against.
//   3. **THE TWO PARK STORES**, under `__sched_lock` — the timer store's count/base/capacity and the process
//      store's count, plus the three swap-removes `__gt_promise_drop` reaches. What is indivisible there is a
//      TRANSACTION over several words, which no atomic can express. The wake is deliberately outside the
//      lock; `emitSchedEnqueueLocked` / `emitSchedWakeAnIdleM` carry that requirement in their names.
//   4. **THE THREE OUT-PARAM SCRATCH REGIONS** (`__gt_trace_digits`, `__gt_clock_ticks`, `__gt_clock_freq`)
//      are FRAME SLOTS now, not `.data` — per-call storage for a per-call need, which also deleted three
//      globals and one `__slab_alloc`.
//   5. **THE ASYNC RELEASER's CLAIM** is a CAS (`GtRuntime.emitGtReleaserCall`), the one item on the list
//      whose failure was a DOUBLE FREE rather than a lost count.
//   6. **THE SUBPROCESS HANDLE TABLE** — its lazy creation under the lock, its per-slot claim by CAS.
//
// ✅ **THE TWO ITEMS THAT WERE STILL RACY ABOVE ONE M ARE BOTH CLOSED.**
//   • **THE ALLOCATOR (S5).** It was ONE shard with no lock and no ownership gate, and
//     `maxon-shv2/track0/alloc-torture.maxon` died at `MAXON_MAX_PROCS=2` and above with exit 86
//     (`slabSpanExhaustedPastItsEnd`, INV-1) or a segfault. S5 sharded it PER P: each processor owns its own
//     mcache row and is the sole writer of every span cached there, every span carries the P that owns it, a
//     free by a P that does not own the span CAS-pushes onto the owner's remote-free queue, and an OS thread
//     with no P uses a dedicated raw row behind a lock. `SlabRuntime.maxon`'s header records the mechanism
//     and `specs-shv2/slab-sharding.md` pins the part one processor can reach.
//     ⚠ **W212 KEEPS ITS PREMISE, WHICH IS THAT A P IS BOUND TO EXACTLY ONE M.** The ring below is per-P and
//     its slots are read by thieves, but the SHARD is untouched: no work in this rung migrates a P between
//     Ms (that is W213), so `owning_p` still answers one M.
//   • **`ClockRuntime`'s `__clock_wall_ticks` and `__thread_cpu_ticks_out` (W212)** — the same out-param
//     scratch shape item 4 fixed here, in a file G2 did not own. The fix is item 4's, applied one file over:
//     `func.stackSize` plus a `StdOp.stackRecordAddr` each, and the two `.data` words deleted.
//
// ⇒ **a green thread that allocates, retains, releases, sleeps, spawns a child, traces or reads any clock is
// safe on a worker M.** What is NOT yet built above one M is P MIGRATION: a green thread that blocks in a
// kernel call holds its P for the duration, because `entersyscall`/`handoffp`/`sysmon` are W213.
//
// ⭐ **WHY A SEPARATE FILE FROM `GtRuntime`.** Not tidiness: the two answer different questions, and one of
// them is read by code the other cannot see. `emitLoadCurrentGt` below is called from the x64 SHIM and the
// x64 PROLOGUE GUARD — hand-assembled Target-tier chunks — as well as from a dozen Std builders, so the P
// layout is a contract shared across the tier boundary exactly as the GT layout is. Buried in `GtRuntime`'s
// 5,900 lines it would read as one more green-thread field.

// ==========================================================================
// ⭐⭐⭐ THE MULTI-M SHARING CLASS OF A RUNTIME `.data` WORD (G2)
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⛔⛔ **A `.data` WORD IS REACHABLE FROM EVERY OS THREAD BY CONSTRUCTION, SO EVERY ONE OF THEM OWES AN
// ANSWER — AND THE ANSWER IS A REQUIRED ARGUMENT RATHER THAN A COMMENT.** G1 left the audit as the prose
// list this file's header used to carry, derived by grepping the tree for *"safe because single-M"*; the
// grep is what found its last four items, which is exactly the property a prose list cannot keep. G2 turns
// it into `pushRuntimeGlobal`'s `sharing` parameter: a slot that nobody has classified is not a slot the
// compiler will build, because the call that creates it does not compile without one.
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS DELIBERATELY NOT A ROSTER + A CHECKER.** A table keyed by label would be a SECOND list of the
// slots, kept in step with the producers by nothing, and its checker would have to run somewhere and could be
// forgotten. The classification travels ON the slot's own creation, which is the shape `StdOpMeta` uses one
// tier up — *"a new variant cannot be added without declaring all of it"* — and it has that shape's property:
// there is no state at which the fact is missing, so there is nothing to check.
export enum MultiMSharing
	// Every mutation is `StdOp.atomicRmw` or `StdOp.atomicCas` IN EVERY BUILD WHERE A SECOND M CAN EXIST.
	// The counters (`MmRuntime.emitGlobalAccumulate`) and the claim words are here.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE QUALIFIER IS EC8's AND IT IS NOT A LOOPHOLE.** `emitGlobalAccumulate` emits a plain
	// load/add/store when `usage.usesGt` is false — a program with no green threads has ONE OS thread, so
	// there is no second writer for an atomic to serialise against and the `lock` prefix is paid on every
	// allocation in the language to guard a race that cannot occur. The class states the discipline a slot
	// owes WHEN THE HAZARD EXISTS; it never claimed a prefix is emitted where no thread can race.
	atomicStep
	// Every read AND every mutation happens inside `__sched_lock`. A word whose transaction spans several
	// words — the FIFO's head/tail pair, the timer store's count/base/capacity triple, the process store's
	// count — cannot be made indivisible by an atomic, because what must be indivisible is not a word.
	underSchedLock
	// Written before any worker M can exist, and never after. Bring-up is single-threaded BY CONSTRUCTION and
	// not by convention: the first `__gt_init` is reached by the only thread there is, because
	// `__sched_init_procs` — which it calls — is what BUILDS the P array a worker is spawned onto, and the
	// spawner (`__sched_wake_or_spawn`) is reachable only from an enqueue, which is reachable only after
	// bring-up returns. A slot nothing writes at all (`__sched_shutdown_flag`, whose teardown does not exist
	// yet) is sealed trivially and rides this class too.
	sealedBeforeWorkers
	// A plain read-modify-write whose lost update is BOUNDED and SELF-CORRECTING, argued at its own site.
	// One member: `__sched_max_active_workers`, a monotone high-water mark — a lost sample loses a transient
	// reading while the mark still converges to the true peak, and it can never UNDER-report two live
	// workers, because the reader's own increment is already visible in the value it compares.
	monotoneApproximation
	// ONE aligned machine word, written whole and read whole, whose contract does not say WHICH writer's
	// value a reader gets. No tearing is possible on any lane shv2 emits, so every observable value is a value
	// somebody really wrote; what multi-M costs is precision, not safety. One member:
	// `__subp_last_error`, whose own site argues why exactness there needs per-green-thread storage.
	lastWriterWins
	// ⛔ **THE ANSWER `pushRuntimeGlobal` REFUSES, and the reason it is a member rather than an omission is
	// that "I do not know" is the answer a new slot arrives with.** A shared word with no discipline is the
	// failure this whole rung exists to remove — a lost update nothing reproduces on demand — so a slot that
	// wants this class is not a slot that may be added: fix it (one of the four above), or take the surface
	// it belongs to as its own rung and put the slot in a file that owns it.
	unclassified
end 'MultiMSharing'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE DOOR A RUNTIME `.data` SLOT IS BORN THROUGH**, and the only place the
// `DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(<label>), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0)` incantation is spelled —
// it was written out at thirty sites across six producers, which is thirty chances to get the type or the
// seed wrong and, until this rung, thirty slots whose sharing nobody had to state.
//
// `seed` defaults to 0 because the zero image IS the initial state for almost every slot (see
// `gtRuntimeGlobals`); the two worker counters are the exception and say so by passing one.
//
// ⚠ **THE `usage`/`--debugstream` GATES STAY AT THE CALL SITES.** Whether a slot is laid out at all is a
// question about the PROGRAM; this door answers a question about the SLOT.
export function pushRuntimeGlobal(out DataSectionEntryArray, label ByteArray, sharing MultiMSharing, seed ParsedInt = 0)
	match sharing 'classified'
		atomicStep to lastWriterWins then break 'classified'
		unclassified then panic("pushRuntimeGlobal: '{String.from(label)}' is laid out with no multi-M sharing class. A `.data` word is reachable from every OS thread by construction, so it needs one of: atomicStep (every mutation is an atomicRmw/atomicCas wherever a second M can exist), underSchedLock (every read and write inside `__sched_lock`), sealedBeforeWorkers (written only during bring-up, which no worker M can race), monotoneApproximation (a plain RMW whose lost update is bounded and self-correcting), or lastWriterWins (one word whose contract never said which writer wins). Argue the choice at the site. See MultiMSharing.")
	end 'classified'

	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(label), valueType: StdType.i64, value: seed))
end 'pushRuntimeGlobal'

// ==========================================================================
// The ProcContext (P) struct
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⭐ **BYTE-IDENTICAL TO `GtLayout.cs:360-402` THROUGH 0x80**, so the bootstrap's debug agent, its allocator
// shard index and its netpoll all graft on without a field moving. Fields this rung does not use are
// RESERVED rather than omitted, for the reason `GtRuntime` reserves the GT's 0x60–0xA0 window: a field added
// later at a different offset is a layout fork, and a layout fork between two compilers that share a
// debugger is a silent wrong answer.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE TOTAL SIZE IS *NOT* THE BOOTSTRAP'S 376 BYTES, AND IT CANNOT BE.** `POffMainThread` holds an
// INLINE GT, and shv2's GT struct is 0x128 where the bootstrap's is 0xE8 — because shv2 carries a
// `releaser`, a `traceId`, an `ioScratch`, a `MaxAsyncArgs`-slot inline argument region, a teardown word, an
// owner + coroutine-queue triple and a park note the bootstrap has no equivalent of (`GtRuntime`'s
// `GtOffArgInline`, `GtOffTeardown`, `GtOffOwner` and `GtOffParkKind` each state why). A struct that embeds
// a bigger struct is bigger. What IS preserved is the property the byte-identity exists for: every offset up
// to and including `POffMainThread` matches, so the header is portable, and only the tail — which grafts
// onto nothing — differs.

// ⭐⭐ **THE PER-P LOCAL RUN-QUEUE RING (Go's `runq`) — TWO MONOTONIC COUNTERS, NOT TWO POINTERS.** They
// count how many green threads have EVER been pushed to and popped from this ring; the ring slot is
// `counter mod RunqSlots` and the length is `tail - head`. That is what makes the pop a single CAS: a thief
// and the owner both advance `runqhead` by compare-and-swap, and a counter that only ever rises has no ABA
// to have (a head that reads `h` twice really did not move — a pair of pointers can look identical after a
// full lap). `POffRunqBase` at the P's tail is the slot array itself.
//
// ⛔ **THERE IS NO LENGTH WORD, AND 0x10 IS THEREFORE STILL RESERVED.** The bootstrap keeps a `len` beside
// its head/tail because its local queue is a LINKED LIST whose length cannot be computed; a ring's is
// `tail - head`, and a third word maintained beside two that already answer the question is a fact stored
// twice — which is exactly how a queue comes to disagree with itself. It stays reserved rather than being
// reused so the bootstrap's debug agent, which reads this header, still finds its own field where it left it.
//
// ⚠ NEITHER IS EXPORTED, AND THE QUESTION THEY ANSWER FOR ANOTHER FILE IS ASKED THROUGH A DOOR IN THIS ONE.
// Both were exported so `GtRuntime`'s yield arm could read them directly; W212 made that question an
// `emitRunqAnyQueued` door, EC10 removed the asker (with no green threads to schedule, a yield could only
// ever be handed to its OWNER's coroutine queue) and **SV1 brought the asker back with `spawn`, which is the
// rung the deletion note named**. It is `emitSchedAnyRunnable` below rather than a re-export, because where
// the ring keeps its counters and what makes it empty belong to the file that owns the P.
let POffRunqHead = 0x00 as ByteOffset
let POffRunqTail = 0x08 as ByteOffset
let POffReservedLocalQueueLen = 0x10 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **THE GREEN THREAD THIS M IS RUNNING** — the field that replaces the single `.data` word shv2 kept it
// in until this rung. A global cannot express it under more than one M by construction: two Ms run two
// different green threads at the same instant, and every reader of *"the current GT"* — the syscall shim,
// the prologue stack guard, `__gt_sleep`, both await drivers, the trace — would read whichever M wrote last.
//
// EXPORTED because those readers span both tiers: builder-built Std here and in `GtRuntime`, and the
// hand-assembled x64 shim and prologue guard.
export let POffCurrentGt = 0x18 as ByteOffset

// This processor's index in `__sched_procs`, `0 .. numProcs-1`. It is also the shard index the sharded
// allocator will key its mcache by (the bootstrap's `POffId` carries the same double duty), which is why it
// is an INDEX rather than a pointer or a thread id.
//
// EXPORTED because that day arrived: the sharded allocator reads it to pick its mcache row.
export let POffId = 0x20 as ByteOffset

// The xorshift state a work-stealing P picks its victim with — its first reader arrived with the stealing
// rounds in `__sched_find_runnable`. Seeded to `id + 1` at init, because a zero xorshift state is a FIXED
// POINT: a P seeded 0 would draw 0 for ever and always try to steal from P[0].
let POffRng = 0x28 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **THE PARK FLAG — one half of the Dekker pair.** 1 means *"this M has published that it is about to
// block on `wakeEvent`"*. Written by the owning M (to 1, when parking; to 0, on every path that resumes) and
// by an ENQUEUER (to 0, immediately before it signals). See `buildSchedWorkerLoop` for why the publish must
// be an ATOMIC read-modify-write and not a plain store.
let POffIdleFlag = 0x30 as ByteOffset

// The auto-reset Win32 event this P's M blocks on when it has no work, and an enqueuer signals to wake it.
// AUTO-reset (`osEventCreate`), so a signal that arrives while the M is still running is not lost — it
// leaves the event signalled and the next `osWaitHandle` returns at once.
let POffWakeEvent = 0x38 as ByteOffset

// The `CreateThread` HANDLE of the worker M running on this P, or 0 for P[0] (the main OS thread, which we
// never created). Stored rather than reserved because the handle is otherwise LOST: a reserved slot would
// mean leaking one kernel handle per worker with no way to reach it. Nothing READS it at this rung; the
// teardown that joins the workers out is a later rung's.
let POffOsThreadHandle = 0x40 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **THE CLAIM WORD — `PStatusUnused` (0) or `PStatusActive` (1).** A P struct exists for every processor
// the program may ever run on, so *"does this P exist"* and *"is an M running on it"* are different
// questions and only this one answers the second. **It is claimed by CAS and never by a plain store** — see
// `emitSchedClaimProc`, which carries the heap corruption that mistake produced in the reference.
let POffStatus = 0x48 as ByteOffset
let PStatusUnused = 0 as ParsedInt
let PStatusActive = 1 as ParsedInt

// ⭐⭐ **THE DEFERRED RE-ENQUEUE SLOTS — the FRONT one.** A green thread handed a wakeup may not be made
// discoverable until `__gt_context_switch` has finished SAVING ITS REGISTERS: between an enqueue and that
// save it is on the run queue *while still executing on its own stack*, so a second M can pop it and resume
// it onto a stale SP — two Ms running one stack. `GtRuntime.maxon` states that window in full, and the C#
// bootstrap hit it as a documented `--workers>=5` crash. The waking side therefore parks the GT HERE and the
// scheduler drains it AFTER the switch.
//
// ⚠ **IT HAS NO PRODUCER, AND ITS END IS REAL AGAIN (SV1).** It drains through `__sched_publish` — the
// SOONEST end there is for a green thread, this P's own ring, which `__sched_find_runnable` consults before
// anything else — where the yielder drains through `__sched_publish_yield` and lands behind the global
// queue. EC10 collapsed the two because a woken thread could only be a COROUTINE, whose owner has exactly
// one FIFO; a `spawn` makes the distinction observable again, and the two doors are where it now lives.
let POffPendingWaiter = 0x50 as ByteOffset

// Go's `runnext` — the one green thread a P runs before consulting its local ring.
//
// ⛔⛔ **RESERVED AND DELIBERATELY NOT BUILT, AND THE REASON IS GO'S OWN.** `proc.go:7530-7541` refuses
// `runnext` outright when the runtime has no `sysmon`: *"A runnext goroutine shares the same time slice as
// the current goroutine … To prevent a ping-pong pair of goroutines from starving all others, we depend on
// sysmon to preempt long-running goroutines … If there is no sysmon, we must avoid runnext entirely or risk
// starvation."* shv2 is cooperative and has no sysmon — that is **W213**, with `entersyscall`/`handoffp` —
// so a `runnext` here would be the starvation Go names, with nothing to break it.
//
// ⚠ **AND SHV2 HAS NO FRONT-OF-QUEUE PRODUCER TO PUT IN IT.** The two deferred slots are what would feed it,
// and only the YIELDER one has a producer — which belongs at the BACK by measurement (see
// `POffPendingYielder`). `POffPendingWaiter`, the slot whose contents WOULD go here, has none at all. The
// slot therefore waits for its consumer AND its safety mechanism, which are the same rung.
let POffRunnext = 0x58 as ByteOffset

// The per-P GT-struct free list (Go's per-P reuse). ⚠ RESERVED AND UNUSED: `__gt_spawn` takes its struct
// from `__slab_alloc`, whose zeroing contract answers the recycling trap a private free list has to answer
// by hand (`GtRuntime.buildGtSpawn` carries that history).
let POffFreeListHead = 0x60 as ByteOffset
let POffFreeListLen = 0x68 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **THIS P's OWN SYSCALL STACK — the field that DELETES a filed multi-M prerequisite rather than
// documenting it.** Every Win32 call a green thread makes runs on a scratch stack instead of the GT's tiny
// one (`X64GtRuntime`'s syscall shim). Until this rung that scratch stack was ONE GLOBAL REGION
// (`__gt_system_stack_top`) whose safety invariant was prose: *"single-M runs one thing at a time"*.
// **Multi-M falsifies that by definition** — two Ms inside shimmed calls concurrently would both set RSP to
// the same top and write the same 64 KB — and the failure shape is silent interleaved corruption, not a
// clean fault. shv2 had copied the SIZE from the bootstrap's PER-P constant and made the REGION global.
//
// Moving it here makes the invariant unrepresentable instead of forbidden: an M can only reach its own P.
// EXPORTED because the shim (hand-assembled) reads it.
export let POffSystemStackSP = 0x70 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **THE HEAD OF THIS P's MPSC REMOTE-FREE QUEUE — a Treiber stack, and it is LIVE.** An M that frees a
// slot whose span another P owns CAS-pushes it here, and the owner drains it on its next slow path
// (`SlabRuntime.maxon`'s header for the mechanism, and its two readers at the push and the drain).
//
// ⛔ **THIS NOTE SAID "RESERVED AND LEFT ZERO — shv2's slab has one shard and no ownership gate" UNTIL THE
// W212 REVIEW, AND S5 HAD FALSIFIED IT.** The sharding landed, gave the slot its two callers, and left the
// sentence here saying the opposite of this file's own header three screens up — one fact written twice,
// with the second copy rotting. A W213 author reading only this line would conclude a P carries no
// cross-thread state at all, which is exactly the premise P migration must NOT be built on.
export let POffRemoteFreeHead = 0x78 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **THIS M's OWN SCHEDULER GREEN THREAD — an INLINE GT struct, not a pointer.** It is Go's `g0`: the
// context the M runs its scheduler loop on, and the `from` half of every `__gt_context_switch` into a real
// green thread. Inline because it is created with the P, lives exactly as long, and a pointer would be a
// second allocation and a second lifetime for a struct that has neither.
//
// Its `stackBase` is 0 (the slab's zero), which is load-bearing three ways: the syscall shim's guard reads
// it as *"this is a real OS thread stack, call directly"*, the prologue stack guard reads it as *"nothing to
// grow"*, and `__gt_runner_done` reads it as *"never free this stack"*. Its TIB bounds are
// captured by the first `__gt_context_switch` AWAY from it, exactly as GT0's are.
//
// ⚠ NOT exported: every reader is in this file (`emitSchedAdoptProc` and the worker loop's run arm), and an
// `export` with no cross-file consumer is exactly what E3092 refuses.
let POffMainThread = 0x80 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **THE DEFERRED RE-ENQUEUE SLOTS — the BACK one, and it may NOT share the front one's slot.**
// `GtLayout.cs:388-402` states why, from a measured failure: a woken awaiter goes to the FRONT, which is
// right; a YIELDER routed the same way lands in the slot it just vacated and the next dequeue hands it
// straight back — *"a thousand yields from one green thread left a sibling that had never run still unrun"*.
// One slot cannot carry both ends of a queue.
//
// ⚠⚠ **ONLY THIS SLOT HAS A PRODUCER TODAY, AND THE TWO DO NOT SHARE ITS SAFETY ARGUMENT.** The YIELDER
// slot is written ONLY by the green thread currently running on this P (`__gt_resched`'s handoff, its one
// writer) and drained ONLY by that same P's own scheduler loop, so no other M can see it and a plain store
// is enough. `POffPendingWaiter` has no producer here yet, and when it gets one it will NOT inherit that
// argument: the bootstrap's is written by whichever M completed the awaited thread, which is the whole
// reason `GtLayout.cs:396-399` marks the single-writer property as the YIELDER's and calls it *"a stronger
// property than POffPendingWaiter has"*. Whoever writes the waiter's producer owes the synchronisation this
// slot does not.
//
// It sits AFTER the inline GT rather than in the header, because the header is full through 0x78 and every
// one of those offsets is the bootstrap's.
let POffPendingYielder = POffMainThread + (GtStructSize as ByteOffset)

// One machine word — the stride of the `P*` array `__sched_procs` points at, the width of every scalar
// field above, and the stride of one ring slot. Distinct from `GtRuntime.StackWordBytes` (a STACK word) and
// `GtArgSlotBytes` (an async ARGUMENT slot) though all three are 8: three facts, three names, so moving one
// cannot move the others.
let PointerSlotBytes = 8 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THIS P's SCHEDULE COUNTER — Go's `schedtick`, and the ONLY thing the fairness check reads.** One up
// per green thread `__sched_find_runnable` hands back, and every 61st of them consults the GLOBAL queue
// before the local ring. Written by the owner alone, so a plain load/add/store: a thief has no reason to
// know how often its victim has scheduled.
let POffSchedTick = POffPendingYielder + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐ **HOW MANY GREEN THREADS THIS P HAS STOLEN — the reading `__Builtins.schedStealCount()` sums.** It is a
// per-P word and not a `.data` counter for the reason the whole rung is arranged around: a `.data` word is
// visible in every golden's `data {` section, so one added here would move the emitted image of every
// green-thread program in the corpus to carry a number nothing but a torture harness reads. Owner-written,
// like `schedTick`, and summed across `__sched_procs` by `__sched_steal_count`.
let POffStealCount = POffSchedTick + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE DEFERRED PARK SLOT — the THIRD deferred-re-enqueue slot, and the one W218 needed (SV1).** The
// two above hold a thread that is RUNNABLE and must not be discoverable until its registers are saved; this
// holds one that is about to WAIT and must not be registered on the store that will wake it until the same
// instant. `GtRuntime.emitGtPark` writes the GT here and switches; `__sched_drain_pending` reads it at the
// scheduler's loop top — after `__gt_context_switch` returned — and calls `__gt_register_park`. What the
// thread is waiting FOR travels on the GT itself (`GtOffParkKind`/`GtOffParkArg`), so this stays one word.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS `POffPendingYielder`'s ARGUMENT EXACTLY, WHICH IS WHY IT IS A PLAIN STORE.** Single-writer (the
// green thread currently running on this P, which is the only thread that can park itself) and single-reader
// (that same P's own scheduler loop, which is what it switched INTO). No other M can see it, so there is
// nothing here for an atomic to serialise against — the stronger property `POffPendingWaiter` does not have.
//
// ⚠ It is a `.data`-free slot for `POffStealCount`'s reason: a `.data` word would appear in the `data {`
// section of every green-thread golden in the corpus.
let POffPendingPark = POffStealCount + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐⭐ **THE RING'S 256 SLOTS, AT THE P's TAIL.** Go's `runq [256]guintptr` (`runtime2.go:807`), the same
// count v1's arm64 backend uses (`Arm64MacosGreenThread.maxon:163`, `GtMaxLocalQueueLen = 256`) and the same
// the bootstrap caps its linked local queue at. FIXED rather than grown: the overflow has somewhere to go
// (the global queue), so a full ring is a routing decision and never an error, and a growable ring would
// need a lock on the one path this whole rung exists to take a lock OFF.
//
// It sits at the TAIL because everything above it is either the bootstrap's layout or an 8-byte scalar, and
// a 2 KB array in the middle would push every one of them past an offset another compiler's debug agent
// already knows.
let RunqSlots = 256 as ParsedInt

// `counter mod RunqSlots` as a mask, which is what makes the slot computation an `and` rather than an
// `idiv`. It is `RunqSlots - 1` and is only a mask because `RunqSlots` is a power of two — stated here so
// that a future `RunqSlots` that is not one fails in ONE place rather than addressing the wrong slot.
let RunqIndexMask = RunqSlots - 1

// ⭐ **HALF A RING — the overflow batch, the steal grab and the global batch are all this number.** Written
// as a SHIFT and not as `RunqSlots / 2` because `/` in this language answers a FLOAT whatever its operands
// are (`n / 2` of two ints is `128.0`), and a constant that has to be truncated at each of its four uses
// would be the same fact converted four times.
let RunqHalfSlots = RunqSlots shr 1

let POffRunqBase = POffPendingPark + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// 0x9B8 = 2488 bytes: the bootstrap's header, shv2's bigger inline GT, four tail scalars and the ring. See
// the layout header for why this is not the bootstrap's 376.
//
// ⚠ **THIS NUMBER IS DERIVED AND THE COMMENT IS NOT, WHICH IS WHY IT HAD DRIFTED.** It read *"0x990 = 2448"*
// from the day shv2's GT struct was 0xF8; EC10 grew that struct to 0x118 and nothing here moved, so the
// comment described a layout no build had produced for two rungs. Every offset above is derived from
// `GtStructSize`, so the CODE was right the whole time — a prose number beside a derived one is the same
// fact written twice, and this is the copy that rots.
let PStructSize = ((POffRunqBase as ParsedInt) + RunqSlots * PointerSlotBytes) as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **HOW OFTEN THE GLOBAL QUEUE IS CONSULTED FIRST — Go's 61 (`proc.go:3457`), and v1's
// (`Arm64MacosGreenThread.maxon:172`, `GtFairnessInterval`).** Without it two green threads that keep
// re-readying each other own the local ring outright and everything the global queue holds starves. A ring
// OVERFLOW is what routinely puts work there, so this is not a corner case: it is the other half of the
// overflow.
//
// ⚠ **shv2 TESTS IT AFTER THE INCREMENT WHERE GO TESTS IT BEFORE**, so the first check falls on the 61st
// schedule rather than on the 0th. Go's tick is bumped in `execute` and read in `findRunnable`, which makes
// its very first schedule a global check; here that would be a special case with no reader, since the global
// queue is empty before anything has overflowed into it. The property both spell is the one that matters —
// nothing waits in the global queue for more than 61 schedules.
let GtFairnessInterval = 61 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **FOUR ROUNDS OF STEALING BEFORE A P GIVES UP — Go's `stealTries` (`proc.go:3849`).** More than one
// because a victim that is momentarily empty may be about to publish; bounded because a P that finds nothing
// four times over should park and let an enqueuer wake it rather than burn a core.
let StealRounds = 4 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **HOW MANY GREEN THREADS ONE GRAB TAKES OFF THE GLOBAL QUEUE — half a ring, which is Go's first term
// (`proc.go:3492`, `globrunqgetbatch(int32(len(pp.runq)) / 2)`).**
//
// ⚠ **GO'S OTHER TWO TERMS ARE NOT TAKEN, AND ONE OF THEM CANNOT BE.** `globrunqgetbatch` takes
// `min(n, size, size/gomaxprocs+1)`: the `size` term is implicit here (the walk stops when the queue
// empties), but `size/gomaxprocs+1` needs the global queue's LENGTH, and shv2's global queue is an intrusive
// list with no length word — adding one would be a `.data` slot, which is the one thing this rung may not
// spend (see `POffStealCount`). What that term buys is FAIRNESS BETWEEN Ps on a queue several Ps are
// draining at once, never correctness: without it a P may take up to half a ring where Go would have left
// some for its peers, and the peers then steal from it. The 61-schedule check is the fairness mechanism that
// actually matters here, and it is present.
let GlobalBatchMax = RunqHalfSlots

// ==========================================================================
// The `.data` slots
// ==========================================================================

// The `TlsAlloc` slot index, kept so a teardown rung can `TlsFree` it, and read once at adoption to publish
// this OS thread's P. The per-read path never touches it — it reads the precomputed byte offset below.
let SchedTlsIndexLabel = b"__sched_tls_index"

// ⭐⭐ **THE PRECOMPUTED TEB BYTE OFFSET, `index * 8 + 0x1480` — what makes the current-P read TWO LOADS AND
// NO CALL.** Windows keeps a thread's first 64 TLS slots inline in its TEB at `TEB + 0x1480`, and `gs:`
// addresses the TEB, so `mov reg, gs:[thisOffset]` IS `TlsGetValue(index)` with no call, no range check and
// no clobbered argument register. Both references precompute exactly this word and for exactly this reason.
//
// ⚠ **ZERO MEANS "THE SCHEDULER HAS NOT INITIALIZED YET", AND EVERY HAND-ASSEMBLED READER MUST TEST IT.**
// Before `__sched_init_procs` runs, `gs:[0]` is `NT_TIB.ExceptionList` — a non-null pointer on Windows — so
// reading through an unchecked zero offset hands back garbage and the next field load faults. The prologue
// stack guard runs inside `main` before any spawn, so that is a reachable path and not a defensive one.
export let SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel = b"__sched_tls_teb_offset"

// The base of the `P*` array — `numProcs` machine words, one pointer per processor.
let SchedProcsLabel = b"__sched_procs"

// ⭐ **HOW MANY Ps EXIST — the bound every scan walks, and the ceiling on live Ms.** The reference keeps TWO
// words here (`__sched_num_procs` beside `__sched_max_procs`) because a P can be retired without shrinking
// the array; shv2 keeps ONE, because nothing retires a P and a second word with no writer is a variable's
// clothes on a constant — the same argument `CpuParallelRuntime` made against a `.data` slot for a `ret 1`.
// The day a P is retired, the pair splits here, in one place.
let SchedNumProcsLabel = b"__sched_num_procs"

// ⭐ **HOW MANY Ms ARE LIVE, AND THE HIGH-WATER MARK OF THAT COUNT.** The mark is what
// `__Builtins.schedMaxActiveWorkers()` answers, which is the only way a program can observe that more than
// one M ever ran. Both are seeded to **1** in `.data` rather than by an initializer — see
// `schedRuntimeGlobals`.
export let SchedActiveWorkersLabel = b"__sched_active_workers"
export let SchedMaxActiveWorkersLabel = b"__sched_max_active_workers"

// Set at teardown so a parked worker leaves its loop instead of blocking for ever. ⚠ The worker loop reads
// it TWICE, because the second read is half of the missed-wakeup argument rather than a duplicate of the
// first. See `buildSchedWorkerLoop`.
//
// ⭐ **IT HAS A WRITER AS OF SV1 (`__sched_stop_workers`), WHICH IS WHY IT MOVED CLASS.** It was
// `sealedBeforeWorkers` on the honest ground that NOTHING wrote it; the teardown does, and it writes it
// AFTER the workers exist, so that class would now be a false statement. `lastWriterWins` is the true one
// and the discipline it names is enough: a single monotone 0→1 store by the main thread, one aligned word,
// and a reader that gets the stale 0 simply parks once more and re-reads at its own timeout.
let SchedShutdownFlagLabel = b"__sched_shutdown_flag"

// ⭐ **THE ONE LOCK OVER THE RUN QUEUE — a `.data` word holding a slab-allocated CRITICAL_SECTION.** It was
// `__io_lock`, allocated by `__io_init` and taken only when the program read a pipe, because the IOCP
// completion thread was then the run queue's only cross-thread mutator. A worker M is a second one, so the
// lock is now allocated by `__gt_init` for EVERY green-thread program and taken at every head/tail mutation.
//
// ⚠ **ONE LOCK RATHER THAN TWO, AND THAT IS A DEADLOCK ARGUMENT.** It also still guards the IOCP `ioParked`
// state machine, which is what it guarded before. Two locks — one for the queue, one for the handshake —
// would have to be held together on the completion thread's enqueue path, and two locks held together have
// an ORDER that two files would each have to remember. A CRITICAL_SECTION is recursive, so one lock covering
// both has no order to get wrong.
export let SchedLockLabel = b"__sched_lock"

// ==========================================================================
// Entry points
// ==========================================================================

// `__sched_init_procs()` — reserve the TLS slot, size and build the P array, and adopt P[0] for the calling
// (main) OS thread. Called once, from `__gt_init`, before that thread does anything else.
export let SchedInitProcsName = b"__sched_init_procs"

// `__sched_worker_loop(p)` — a worker M's whole life. The `lpParameter` `osThreadCreate` hands it IS its P.
let SchedWorkerLoopName = b"__sched_worker_loop"

// `__sched_wake_or_spawn()` — hand a newly-runnable green thread to an idle M, or start one on a free P.
//
// ⭐ **EXPORTED AGAIN AT SV1, AND ITS TWO CROSS-FILE READERS ARE BACK FOR THE REASON THEY LEFT.** EC10 took
// them away because an `async` frame is a coroutine and a coroutine may not be handed to a worker M; a
// `spawn` creates a GREEN THREAD, which is exactly what the ring, the stealing and the worker loop schedule.
// The readers are the hand-assembled IOCP completion loop (which wakes after publishing a green thread) and
// the `DeadFunctionElimination` root that keeps this alive for that call.
export let SchedWakeOrSpawnName = b"__sched_wake_or_spawn"

// `__sched_drain_pending()` — move whatever the three deferred slots hold onto the queue or the store they
// belong on, now that the context switch which made deferral necessary has completed.
export let SchedDrainPendingName = b"__sched_drain_pending"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE THREE PUBLISH DOORS (SV1) — and what they answer is a question about the GT, not about the
// caller.** A COROUTINE goes to its owning green thread's queue; a GREEN THREAD goes to a tier the P/M half
// schedules, and its publish owes a WAKE. `GtRuntime.emitGtIsGreenThread` is the one test, so a publish
// cannot route a thread to the wrong tier by assuming what its caller made.
//
//   • **`__sched_publish(gt)`** — a WAKEUP: green → this P's ring (the soonest place there is, since
//     `__sched_find_runnable` consults it first) plus the wake; coroutine → the owner's queue, no wake.
//     `__gt_ready` and the drain's waiter slot call it.
//   • **`__sched_publish_locked(gt) -> 1 if a green thread was published`** — the same routing for a caller
//     that ALREADY HOLDS `__sched_lock` (the timer walk, the process walk, the IOCP completion loop, the
//     register's overflow arm). It never wakes: a nested `LeaveCriticalSection` releases nothing, so the
//     StoreLoad fence the Dekker pair needs would not exist inside the caller's critical section. It
//     ANSWERS the debt instead, and the caller pays it once after its own release.
//   • **`__sched_publish_yield(gt)`** — a YIELD: green → the GLOBAL queue's tail, which is Go's `Gosched`
//     (a yielder belongs behind everything runnable, not in the slot it just vacated); coroutine → the
//     owner's queue, whose tail is already both.
//
// ⚠ Only the first two are exported: `__gt_ready` calls one and `GtRuntime`'s two store walks call the
// other, while the yield door's only caller is `emitSchedDrainSlot` in this file — which is what E3092
// refuses an `export` for.
export let SchedPublishName = b"__sched_publish"
export let SchedPublishLockedName = b"__sched_publish_locked"
let SchedPublishYieldName = b"__sched_publish_yield"

// ⭐ `__sched_stop_workers()` — seal the scheduler and JOIN every worker M out. Called once, from
// `GtRuntime.__gt_drain_at_exit`, on the exit path: set `__sched_shutdown_flag`, signal every P's wake event
// so a parked M leaves its `osWaitHandle`, then wait on each worker's thread handle. EXPORTED for that one
// cross-file caller — the walk is over `__sched_procs` and the fields are this file's, so the function is
// here and the exit path merely asks for it.
export let SchedStopWorkersName = b"__sched_stop_workers"

// `__sched_runq_put(gt)` — push a runnable green thread onto the CURRENT P's ring tail, overflowing half the
// ring plus `gt` to the global queue when the ring is full. Go's `runqput` + `runqputslow`.
//
// ⚠ NOT exported: `GtRuntime` reaches it through `emitSchedRunqPut` rather than by name, so the SYMBOL has
// no cross-file reader.
let SchedRunqPutName = b"__sched_runq_put"

// `__sched_runq_get(p) -> gt` — take the oldest thread off `p`'s ring by CAS on its head, or 0. Go's
// `runqget`. It takes `p` rather than reading TLS because the SWEEP calls it for the P it already holds.
let SchedRunqGetName = b"__sched_runq_get"

// `__sched_steal(p, victim) -> gt` — grab half of `victim`'s ring into `p`'s and answer one of them, or 0.
// Go's `runqgrab` + `runqsteal`.
let SchedStealName = b"__sched_steal"

// ⭐⭐ `__sched_find_runnable() -> gt` — **THE ONE PLACE THE SCHEDULER DECIDES WHAT RUNS NEXT**, and 0 when
// there is nothing. Go's `findRunnable`, minus everything shv2 has no equivalent of.
//
// ⚠ NOT exported, for `SchedRunqPutName`'s reason: its three drivers reach it through
// `emitSchedFindRunnable`.
let SchedFindRunnableName = b"__sched_find_runnable"

// `__sched_sweep_dropped()` — reclaim the dropped green threads sitting at the FRONT of this P's ring and of
// the global queue.
//
// ⭐ **EXPORTED AGAIN AT SV1.** Its caller is `__gt_promise_drop`, which renounces a promise — and what a
// promise names is a COROUTINE (tombstone in its owner's queue, swept by `__gt_coro_sweep_dropped`) or a
// GREEN THREAD (tombstone in a ring or in the global queue, swept here). The drop asks which and calls one
// of the two; EC10 could drop this half because nothing produced a green thread.
export let SchedSweepDroppedName = b"__sched_sweep_dropped"

// ⭐ `__sched_steal_count() -> i64` — the sum of every P's steal counter, and the floor of
// `__Builtins.schedStealCount()`. It is the only way a Maxon program can observe that work stealing
// happened, which is what `maxon-shv2/track0/steal-torture.maxon` asserts on.
//
// ⚠ **IT LIVES HERE RATHER THAN BESIDE `__sched_max_active_workers` IN `CpuParallelRuntime`, AND THE SPLIT
// IS THAT FILE'S OWN TEST APPLIED HONESTLY.** That file holds the queries whose answer costs no scheduler —
// its `__sched_max_active_workers` is a `.data` load that is TRUE (1) for a program with no scheduler at
// all. A steal count is not: it walks `__sched_procs`, which only `__sched_init_procs` builds, so the
// question has no answer without the machinery. `RuntimeUsage.recordCallUsage` therefore routes it to
// `usesGt` — asking it installs the scheduler, because asking it is a question ABOUT the scheduler.
export let SchedStealCountName = b"__sched_steal_count"


// ==========================================================================
// The TLS read — the one door to "which P am I on"
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **P FOR THE CALLING OS THREAD, OR 0 — TWO LOADS AND NO CALL.** `globalAddr` + `loadIndirect` fetch the
// precomputed TEB offset; `tlsSlotLoad` dereferences it through `gs:`. Every Std caller here runs after
// `__sched_init_procs`, so none needs the zero test the two hand-assembled callers make explicitly (see
// `SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel`).
export function emitLoadCurrentP(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let offsetAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.globalAddr(offsetAddr, label: SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel))
	let tebOffset = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: offsetAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let p = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.tlsSlotLoad(p, tebOffsetId: tebOffset))
	return p
end 'emitLoadCurrentP'

// ⭐ **THE GREEN THREAD THIS M IS RUNNING.** The one door every `GtRuntime` reader goes through, replacing
// the `globalAddr __gt_current_gt` + `loadIndirect` pair it used to be. Stated once here so the P
// indirection cannot be spelled differently at two of a dozen sites.
//
// ⛔⛔ **ITS PRECONDITION IS THAT THE SCHEDULER HAS INITIALIZED, AND THAT IS A REAL OBLIGATION RATHER THAN A
// FORMALITY — IT WAS MEASURED AS A SEGFAULT.** The word this replaced was a `.data` slot that read 0 before
// `__gt_init`, so a pre-init reader got a harmless zero; this reads `gs:[tebOffset]`, and before
// `__sched_init_procs` that offset is 0, which addresses `NT_TIB.ExceptionList` — a NON-NULL pointer — so
// the `currentGt` load lands on `ExceptionList + 0x18` and the process dies. **MEASURED: a `--debugstream`
// green-thread program that logs one event before its first `async`, run under `maxon monitor`, went from
// exit 42 to a SEGMENTATION FAULT** (`1 abandoned (producer died mid-entry)`).
//
// Every `GtRuntime` caller satisfies the precondition because the entry points that reach them all open with
// a lazy `__gt_init` — `__gt_spawn`, `__gt_sleep`, `__gt_resched` and `__gt_io_park` each call it as their
// first act, which is the discipline that made this safe before and still does. A caller that CANNOT make
// that claim takes `emitLoadCurrentGtOrZero` below instead.
export function emitLoadCurrentGt(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: emitLoadCurrentP(module, block: block, ids: ids), offset: POffCurrentGt, ids: ids)
end 'emitLoadCurrentGt'

// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME READ FOR A CALLER THAT MAY RUN BEFORE THE SCHEDULER EXISTS — or on an OS thread that owns
// no P.** Answers the running green thread, or 0. It costs two branches, which is why it is not the door
// above: every scheduler-internal reader has already run `__gt_init` and would pay them for nothing.
//
// **THE TWO ZEROS ARE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS AND BOTH MUST BE ASKED**, exactly as the hand-assembled twin in
// `X64GtRuntime.emitLoadCurrentPOrBail` states: a zero TEB OFFSET means `__sched_init_procs` has not run (and
// `gs:[0]` is not null, so reading through it is a wild load); a zero P means this OS thread owns none, which
// the IOCP completion thread does for its whole life.
//
// It terminates `cur` and returns the block to CONTINUE IN, whose single block arg is the answer — the shape
// every branching emit helper in this runtime uses, because the Std tier has no select.
export function emitLoadCurrentGtOrZero(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, gtId ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var haveOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gtoffset")
	var haveProc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gtproc")
	var merge = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gtmerge")
	merge.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(gtId, argType: StdType.i64))

	let offsetAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.globalAddr(offsetAddr, label: SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel))
	let tebOffset = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: offsetAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let curZero = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let noScheduler = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: tebOffset, b: curZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(noScheduler, thenBlockId: merge.id, elseBlockId: haveOffset.id))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(curZero)))

	let p = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(haveOffset, op: StdOp.tlsSlotLoad(p, tebOffsetId: tebOffset))
	let offsetZero = emitConst(module, block: haveOffset, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let noProc = emitCmp(module, block: haveOffset, a: p, b: offsetZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveOffset, op: StdOp.condBranch(noProc, thenBlockId: merge.id, elseBlockId: haveProc.id))
	haveOffset.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(offsetZero)))

	let gt = emitLoadField(module, block: haveProc, record: p, offset: POffCurrentGt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveProc, op: StdOp.branch(merge.id))
	haveProc.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(gt)))

	return merge
end 'emitLoadCurrentGtOrZero'

// ⚠ **THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `emitStoreCurrentGt` TWIN.** The only writer is `GtRuntime.emitGtRunOne`,
// which already HOLDS the P — it is handed one — so a helper that re-read TLS to store through it would be a
// second TLS read on the dispatch path, dressed as symmetry. A one-line `emitStoreField(record: p, offset:
// POffCurrentGt, …)` at a site that has `p` is the right spelling, and it is what that site uses.

// ⚠ **AND NO Std-TIER READER OF `POffSystemStackSP` EITHER.** Both of its readers — the syscall shim and the
// prologue stack guard's cold path — are hand-assembled Target-tier chunks that reach the field directly;
// nothing in builder-built Std switches a stack. An exported helper here would have been a second spelling
// with no caller, which reads to the next person as though the field had a Std consumer it does not have.

// ==========================================================================
// `.data` and installation
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐ **DEFAULT ONE M. See this file's header** — the machinery below is built, proven and not turned on, and
// this constant is where that decision physically lives.
let DefaultMaxProcs = 1 as ParsedInt

// The environment variable that raises the ceiling for a deliberate multi-M run. ⚠ The bootstrap's spelling
// (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:3601-3631`), taken so a harness that drives both compilers needs one knob.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT CLAMPS AGAINST THE MACHINE, WHERE THE REFERENCE CLAMPS AGAINST THE DETECTED COUNT — and the
// difference is which number is the DEFAULT.** The bootstrap defaults to the processor count and lets the
// variable only LOWER it; shv2 defaults to 1, so a variable that could only lower would have nothing to
// lower to and the structure below could never be exercised at all. The ceiling neither may exceed is the
// same: the machine's own processor count, because a P nothing can ever run on is a P nobody can schedule.
let MaxProcsEnvName = b"MAXON_MAX_PROCS"

// The scratch a `GetEnvironmentVariableA` for a decimal count needs. Far more than any processor count
// spells, so the "buffer too small" answer is unreachable for a well-formed value; a value that overflows it
// is not a processor count, and the read DECLINES rather than retrying.
let MaxProcsEnvBufBytes = 32 as ParsedInt

// The byte offset of a thread's first 64 TLS slots within its TEB. `gs:` addresses the TEB on Win64, so
// `gs:[WindowsTebTlsSlotsOffset + index*8]` IS `TlsGetValue(index)` for those slots — which is the whole
// reason the read costs no call. Both references bake exactly this number.
let WindowsTebTlsSlotsOffset = 0x1480 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE PARK TIMEOUT, IN MILLISECONDS — v1's adaptive pair, and it is a MISSED-WAKEUP SAFETY NET rather
// than a schedule.** A parked M is normally woken by `SetEvent`; the timeout bounds how long a wakeup that
// went missing can wedge it. **2 ms when a sleep timer is pending**, so a deadline fires promptly even if no
// enqueue happens to signal this P, and **100 ms otherwise**, which is idle. `X64Backend.maxon:9310-9316`.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT WHAT MAKES THE PARK CORRECT, AND MUST NOT BE READ AS THOUGH IT WERE.** The Dekker pair in
// `buildSchedWorkerLoop` is what closes the missed-wakeup window; this bounds the damage if that argument is
// ever wrong. A sabotage that breaks the pair therefore shows up as LATENCY at these values and as a HANG
// only with the timeout removed — which is exactly how the ordering was verified.
let ParkTimeoutWithTimersMs = 2 as ParsedInt
let ParkTimeoutIdleMs = 100 as ParsedInt

// The scheduler's `.data` slots. TWO gates rather than one: the worker COUNTERS are readable by a program
// that never spawns a green thread (`__Builtins.schedMaxActiveWorkers()`), while the P machinery is
// meaningless without one.
export function schedRuntimeGlobals(usage RuntimeUsage) returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()

	// ⭐ **SEEDED TO 1 IN `.data`, NOT BY AN INITIALIZER.** A program with no scheduler runs on exactly one M
	// — its own — so the true answer is already in the slot and costs no code. It also removes an ordering
	// question a runtime seed would create: `__Builtins.schedMaxActiveWorkers()` may be called before the
	// first `async`, i.e. before `__gt_init` has run at all.
	if usage.usesSchedMaxActiveWorkers or usage.usesGt 'workerCounters'
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedActiveWorkersLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.atomicStep, seed: 1)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedMaxActiveWorkersLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.monotoneApproximation, seed: 1)
	end 'workerCounters'

	if usage.usesGt 'procs'
		// Five of these six are written by `__sched_init_procs` and by nothing else. The exception is
		// `__sched_shutdown_flag`, whose one writer is the teardown and therefore runs AFTER the workers —
		// see its own declaration for why that makes it `lastWriterWins` rather than sealed.
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedTlsIndexLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedProcsLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedShutdownFlagLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.lastWriterWins)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedLockLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
	end 'procs'

	return out
end 'schedRuntimeGlobals'

// Install the P/M half of the scheduler. Called from `installGtRuntime`, so it rides `usesGt` exactly as the
// green-thread half does — there is no program that wants one without the other.
export function installSchedRuntime(module StdModule)
	buildSchedInitProcs(module)
	buildSchedDrainPending(module)
	// The three publish doors, from two bodies: the two unlocked ones differ only in WHICH END a green
	// thread's publish uses, so they share `buildSchedPublish`; the locked one answers a wake debt instead of
	// performing a wake, which is a different signature and therefore its own body.
	buildSchedPublish(module, name: SchedPublishName, greenEnd: SchedGreenEnd.localRing)
	buildSchedPublish(module, name: SchedPublishYieldName, greenEnd: SchedGreenEnd.globalTail)
	buildSchedPublishLocked(module)
	buildSchedRunqPut(module)
	buildSchedRunqGet(module)
	buildSchedSteal(module)
	buildSchedFindRunnable(module)
	buildSchedSweepDropped(module)
	buildSchedStealCount(module)
	buildSchedWorkerLoop(module)
	buildSchedWakeOrSpawn(module)
	buildSchedStopWorkers(module)
end 'installSchedRuntime'

// --- Shared single-op emitters ---

// The address of `__sched_procs[index]`.
function emitProcSlotAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, index ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let procs = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedProcsLabel, ids: ids)
	return emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: block, base: procs, index: index, ids: ids)
end 'emitProcSlotAddr'

// `__sched_procs[index]` — the P struct at that index.
function emitLoadProc(module StdModule, block IrBlock, index ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: emitProcSlotAddr(module, block: block, index: index, ids: ids), offset: 0, ids: ids)
end 'emitLoadProc'

// Take / release the one run-queue lock. Both are two ops, and both are spelled here so `GtRuntime`'s four
// mutation sites and this file's two reach the same word the same way.
export function emitSchedLockEnter(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osLockEnter(emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedLockLabel, ids: ids)))
end 'emitSchedLockEnter'

export function emitSchedLockLeave(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osLockLeave(emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedLockLabel, ids: ids)))
end 'emitSchedLockLeave'

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH END A GREEN THREAD'S PUBLISH USES — the ONLY thing `__sched_publish` and
// `__sched_publish_yield` differ in, and a real difference rather than a copy's accident.** A woken thread
// belongs at the SOONEST place there is, because it is what somebody was waiting for; a YIELDER belongs
// behind everything runnable, or it lands in the slot it just vacated and the next schedule hands it
// straight back (`GtLayout.cs:388-402`, from the bootstrap's own measurement). Go draws the same line:
// `goready` → `runqput`, `Gosched` → `globrunqput`.
//
// ⚠ A COROUTINE has no such choice: one FIFO per owner, so *"the soonest place"* and *"behind everything
// runnable"* are the same place and the tail is the one that does not hand a yielder back.
enum SchedGreenEnd
	localRing
	globalTail
end 'SchedGreenEnd'

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO ANSWERS `__sched_publish_locked` GIVES, AND WHAT EACH OBLIGES OF ITS CALLER.** The answer
// is not "did it work" — a publish cannot fail — it is *"do you owe a `__sched_wake_or_spawn` once you have
// released the lock?"*, which is true exactly when a GREEN THREAD was published. `emitSchedWakeIfOwed` is
// the one reader, and these are named rather than spelled 1 and 0 because a bare literal at either end
// would be the same fact written twice with nothing tying the two spellings together.
let SchedPublishOwesWake = 1 as ParsedInt
let SchedPublishOwesNothing = 0 as ParsedInt

// ⭐⭐⭐ **PUBLISH A RUNNABLE GT — THE THREE DOORS, AND WHICH QUEUE IS THE *GT's* QUESTION (SV1).** See
// `SchedPublishName` for the three and what each is for. Until this rung the queue was an argument
// (`SchedQueueEnd`), decided by the CALL SITE and answered by a compile-time `match`; a `spawn` makes both
// kinds of GT reachable from the same sites, so the site can no longer know, and the GT can:
// `emitGtIsGreenThread` reads `owner == gt`, which is the relation `GtOffOwner` already carries.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AND THE COROUTINE ARM MUST NOT TAKE THE WAKE — THAT IS WHAT THE BRANCH DISCRIMINATES (EC10).** A
// worker M cannot run a coroutine: it is driven only by its owner's chain of drivers, which is running on
// the M that just published it. So waking an idle M for one is a park/unpark that finds nothing, and
// `__sched_wake_or_spawn`'s SPAWN arm would start an OS thread that idles for the life of the process —
// which `track0/pin-matrix.sh` reads directly, as `workers`.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE STORELOAD FENCE ARGUMENT BELONGS TO THE TWO GREEN-THREAD ARMS AND IS STATED WITH THEM.** The
// Dekker pair in `buildSchedWakeOrSpawn` needs the queue publish to be visible before the `idleFlag` reads,
// and x86 reorders a plain store past a later load; the global tail's fence is the LOCK RELEASE and the
// ring's is the tail's own `lock xadd`. The coroutine arm has no wake, so it has no such pair and no fence
// to name.
function buildSchedPublish(module StdModule, name ByteArray, greenEnd SchedGreenEnd)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var greenArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"publishgreen")
	var coroArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"publishcoro")
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"publishdone")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let gt = 0 as ValueId

	let isGreen = emitGtIsGreenThread(module, block: entry, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isGreen, thenBlockId: greenArm.id, elseBlockId: coroArm.id))

	match greenEnd 'greenEndOf'
		localRing then emitSchedPublishToRing(module, block: greenArm, gt: gt, ids: ids)
		globalTail then emitSchedPublishToGlobalTail(module, block: greenArm, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	end 'greenEndOf'
	module.setTerminator(greenArm, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	emitGtPublishToOwner(module, block: coroArm, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(coroArm, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedPublish'

// ⭐⭐ **THE LOCKED PUBLISH — the same routing for a caller that already holds `__sched_lock`, and it
// ANSWERS the wake rather than performing it (SV1).** See `SchedPublishLockedName` for its four callers and
// why a wake inside a caller's critical section would have no fence. The answer is 1 exactly when a GREEN
// THREAD was published, which is exactly when a wake is owed.
function buildSchedPublishLocked(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedPublishLockedName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var greenArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"publishgreen")
	var coroArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"publishcoro")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let gt = 0 as ValueId

	let isGreen = emitGtIsGreenThread(module, block: entry, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isGreen, thenBlockId: greenArm.id, elseBlockId: coroArm.id))

	// ⚠ The ring push takes no lock of its own and its OVERFLOW takes `__sched_lock` recursively, which is
	// safe and which `buildSchedRunqPut` argues at the acquire. A P-less caller (the IOCP completion thread)
	// reaches the global-queue arm inside that function instead — see its `noProc` test.
	emitSchedRunqPut(module, block: greenArm, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: greenArm, value: SchedPublishOwesWake, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(coroArm, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtCoroEnqueueName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	emitConstReturn(module, block: coroArm, value: SchedPublishOwesNothing, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedPublishLocked'

// The RING tier's publish plus its wake. The fence between the two is `emitRunqPublishTail`'s `lock xadd`,
// which that function states at the instruction; there is no lock here to release and therefore none to be
// the fence.
function emitSchedPublishToRing(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSchedRunqPut(module, block: block, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	emitSchedWakeAnIdleM(module, block: block, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedPublishToRing'

// The GLOBAL tier's publish plus its wake, lock and all. The wake sits AFTER the release because that
// release IS the StoreLoad fence the Dekker pair needs; moving it inside the critical section would be a
// silent correctness regression rather than a scope change.
function emitSchedPublishToGlobalTail(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	emitSchedEnqueueLocked(module, block: block, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	emitSchedWakeAnIdleM(module, block: block, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedPublishToGlobalTail'

// A COROUTINE onto its owning green thread's queue, and no wake. `__gt_coro_enqueue` requires its caller to
// hold `__sched_lock` (the IOCP completion thread appends to the same queue), so this arm takes it; a caller
// that already holds it reaches `__sched_publish_locked` instead, which is the whole reason that door exists.
function emitGtPublishToOwner(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtCoroEnqueueName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: block, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtPublishToOwner'

// ⭐ **THE LOCKED PUBLISH AS ONE CALL, ANSWERING THE WAKE DEBT.** The three walks that publish inside their
// own critical section (`__gt_timer_check`, `__gt_proc_check`, `__gt_register_park`'s overflow arm) reach it
// through this rather than spelling the callee, exactly as `emitSchedRunqPut` and `emitSchedEnqueueLocked`
// are reached — so the lock requirement travels with the name at every site.
export function emitSchedPublishLocked(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let published = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(published, callee: SchedPublishLockedName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	return published
end 'emitSchedPublishLocked'

// ⭐⭐ **PAY A WAKE DEBT, AFTER THE RELEASE AND ONLY IF ONE IS OWED.** `owed` is what
// `emitSchedPublishLocked` answered; `wake` and `cont` are the caller's blocks, so each keeps its own block
// order. Two properties, and neither is optional:
//
//   • **AFTER the release**, because `LeaveCriticalSection` is the StoreLoad fence
//     `buildSchedWakeOrSpawn`'s `idleFlag` scan depends on, and a nested leave releases nothing.
//   • **ONLY IF OWED**, because an unconditional wake in a coroutine-only program would find no idle M and
//     then START one on a free P — an OS thread that idles for the life of the process, which is exactly the
//     `workers` reading `track0/pin-matrix.sh` pins at 1.
export function emitSchedWakeIfOwed(module StdModule, cur IrBlock, owed ValueId, wake IrBlock, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let owesNothing = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: SchedPublishOwesNothing, ids: ids)
	let nothingOwed = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: owed, b: owesNothing, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingOwed, thenBlockId: cont.id, elseBlockId: wake.id))
	emitSchedWakeAnIdleM(module, block: wake, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(wake, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitSchedWakeIfOwed'

// ⭐⭐ **THE GLOBAL QUEUE'S APPEND, AND THE WAKE, SPLIT OUT SO A *BATCH* PUBLISHER CAN COMPOSE THEM
// DIFFERENTLY (G2), AND NAMED FOR THE LOCK STATE EACH REQUIRES.** A caller that already holds
// `__sched_lock` cannot call the door above: its unlock would be a recursive decrement that RELEASES
// NOTHING — deleting the StoreLoad fence the wake depends on, which is a missed wakeup and, with an untimed
// park, a hang. Such a caller appends through the first half inside its loop and calls the second ONCE
// after the release.
//
// ⚠ The function names carry the requirement because nothing else can: `osLockEnter` is a Win32
// CRITICAL_SECTION and therefore RECURSIVE, so taking it twice is not an error and getting this wrong
// produces no diagnostic anywhere — only a wake that observes a stale `idleFlag`.
//
// ⚠ **BOTH ITS CALLERS ARE IN THIS FILE, SO IT IS NOT EXPORTED.** They are the ring's OVERFLOW
// (`buildSchedRunqPut`, moving half a full ring to the global queue under the lock) and
// `emitSchedPublishToGlobalTail`, the arm a YIELDING GREEN THREAD takes. A caller that publishes a COROUTINE
// under a lock it already holds goes through `__sched_publish_locked` instead, which is where the
// recursive-entry problem is answered once.
function emitSchedEnqueueLocked(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtEnqueueName, args: argsOf(gt)))
end 'emitSchedEnqueueLocked'

// ⚠ **NOT EXPORTED: every caller is in this file** — the two green-thread publish arms and
// `emitSchedWakeIfOwed`, which is how a caller that published under its own lock pays the debt afterwards. A
// COROUTINE publish deliberately does not wake at all; `buildSchedPublish` says why waking an M that cannot
// run one is a park/unpark at best and a permanently idle OS thread at worst.
function emitSchedWakeAnIdleM(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SchedWakeOrSpawnName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
end 'emitSchedWakeAnIdleM'

// ⭐ **THE RING PUSH WITHOUT THE WAKE.** Unlike its global twin it requires NO lock state of its caller: the
// ring push takes no lock, and an overflow's recursive entry into `__sched_lock` is safe (see
// `buildSchedRunqPut`).
//
// ⚠ **NOT EXPORTED: both callers are in this file** — `emitSchedPublishToRing` and
// `__sched_publish_locked`'s green arm. The walks that publish inside their own critical section
// (`__gt_timer_check`, `__gt_proc_check`) reach the second of those by name rather than this emitter, so the
// lock requirement travels with the door rather than with the push.
function emitSchedRunqPut(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SchedRunqPutName, args: argsOf(gt)))
end 'emitSchedRunqPut'

// ⭐⭐ **ASK THE SCHEDULER WHICH GREEN THREAD RUNS NEXT — the CONSUME door, and the reason it is one door is
// that the answer is a POLICY** (fairness, stealing, the dropped-thread refusal) rather than a queue read.
//
// ⭐ **EXPORTED AGAIN AT SV1, AND ITS TWO CROSS-FILE CALLERS ARE THE DRIVERS' GREEN-THREAD FALLBACK.**
// `GtRuntime`'s `__gt_drive_until` and `__gt_resched`'s GT0 arm each ask their OWN coroutine queue first —
// an `async` frame never enters any of the three tiers this consults — and then ask this, which is what
// makes P0 run GREEN THREADS cooperatively at the default `MAXON_MAX_PROCS=1`. Without that second question
// a green thread published to a ring at N=1 would sit there for ever: nothing else consults a ring but a
// worker M, and there is no second P for `__sched_wake_or_spawn` to start one on.
export function emitSchedFindRunnable(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let g = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(g, callee: SchedFindRunnableName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	return g
end 'emitSchedFindRunnable'

// ⭐⭐ **TAKE THE NEXT GREEN THREAD OFF THE GLOBAL QUEUE, OR 0 — the CONSUME twin of the append above, and it
// is one door for the same reason.** Popping mutates head AND tail, which every other M and the IOCP
// completion thread also mutate, so it is exactly as lock-bound as the append is.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE FOUR HAND COPIES HAD ALREADY DIVERGED.** Three sites spelled lock/dequeue/unlock
// out (`__gt_drive_until`'s drive, and the worker loop's two) and the fourth — `__gt_resched`'s GT0 arm —
// spelled the dequeue with NO LOCK AT ALL, which it had done since before there was a second M, so it was
// already racing the completion thread in any pipe-reading program that also calls `Runtime.yield()`. That is
// the divergence a copied idiom predicts, and it is a silent wrong answer rather than a compile error: a
// concurrent pop and append can leave the FIFO's tail pointing at a node the head no longer reaches.
//
// ⚠ **SINCE W212 THOSE FOUR SITES ARE ONE — `__sched_find_runnable`, which every driver calls — SO THIS DOOR
// IS NO LONGER EXPORTED EITHER.** It serves the two places inside that function which consult the global
// queue: the 61-schedule fairness check and the sweep. The divergence above is what a single decision point
// makes structurally impossible.
function emitSchedDequeueLocked(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	let g = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(g, callee: GtDequeueName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	return g
end 'emitSchedDequeueLocked'

// ⭐⭐ **HAND THE SCHEDULER A GREEN THREAD TO RE-QUEUE ONCE IT IS OFF ITS OWN STACK.** The producer for
// `POffPendingYielder`, and the reason both slots exist. Its one caller is `__gt_resched`'s handoff arm —
// a green thread that is still RUNNABLE, is about to context-switch to its driver, and must not be
// discoverable until that switch has saved its registers.
//
// It is a plain store because the slot is per-P and single-writer: only the green thread currently running on
// this P writes it, and only that same P's scheduler loop drains it (`__sched_drain_pending`). No other M can
// see it, which is the property `POffPendingYielder`'s note calls stronger than the waiter slot's.
export function emitSchedDeferYielder(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: emitLoadCurrentP(module, block: block, ids: ids), offset: POffPendingYielder, value: gt)
end 'emitSchedDeferYielder'

// ⭐⭐ **HAND THE SCHEDULER A GREEN THREAD TO REGISTER ONCE IT IS OFF ITS OWN STACK (W218).** The producer for
// `POffPendingPark`, and the twin of the yielder's above: its one caller is `GtRuntime.emitGtPark`, from a
// green thread that has decided to WAIT, has written what it is waiting for onto its own struct, and is
// about to context-switch to its driver.
//
// It is a plain store for `emitSchedDeferYielder`'s reason, which this slot has in the same strength: only
// the green thread currently running on this P writes it (a thread can only park ITSELF), and only that same
// P's scheduler loop drains it. No other M can see it.
export function emitSchedDeferPark(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: emitLoadCurrentP(module, block: block, ids: ids), offset: POffPendingPark, value: gt)
end 'emitSchedDeferPark'

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_init_procs`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_init_procs()` — the whole of scheduler bring-up that is about Ms rather than about green
// threads.** Ports the C# `EmitSchedInit` (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:3399`) and v1's `emitX64GtInit`
// (`X64Backend.maxon:9000`), restricted to what a single-M-by-default rung needs and expressed in
// builder-built Std rather than hand-assembled bytes — so it gets register allocation, a checked frame and
// the leak-safe backend for free, exactly as `MmRuntime`'s reasoning one tier up.
//
// In order: reserve the TLS slot and publish its TEB offset; decide how many Ps to build; allocate the run-
// queue lock and the `P*` array; build each P; adopt P[0] for this OS thread.
//
// ⚠ **THE RUN-QUEUE LOCK IS ALLOCATED HERE AND NOT IN `__io_init`.** It guards the queue for every M, not
// only for the IOCP completion thread, so a green-thread program that never touches a pipe must still have
// it. See `SchedLockLabel`.
function buildSchedInitProcs(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedInitProcsName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var settled = module.addBlock(func, label: b"procsettled")
	var loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ploop")
	var loopBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pbody")
	var adopt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"adopt")

	// The TLS slot, and the `index * 8 + 0x1480` byte offset every later read indexes `gs:` by.
	let tlsIndex = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.tlsAlloc(tlsIndex))
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedTlsIndexLabel, value: tlsIndex, ids: ids)
	let slotStride = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: PointerSlotBytes, ids: ids)
	let slotBytes = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: tlsIndex, b: slotStride, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let tebBase = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: WindowsTebTlsSlotsOffset, ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel, value: emitBin(module, block: entry, a: slotBytes, b: tebBase, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids), ids: ids)

	// How many Ps: `DefaultMaxProcs` unless `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` names more, never above the machine's count.
	// The answer arrives as `settled`'s block arg, because the decision is a four-way merge.
	let numProcs = ids.mint()
	settled.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(numProcs, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitResolveMaxProcs(module, func: func, cur: entry, settled: settled, ids: ids)

	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: settled, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, value: numProcs, ids: ids)

	// The run-queue CRITICAL_SECTION, in a slab region whose pointer the `.data` word holds. Its lifetime is
	// the process's, exactly as the completion port's and the wake events' are — see `SchedLockLabel`.
	let lockBytes = emitConst(module, block: settled, value: CriticalSectionBytes, ids: ids)
	let lock = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(settled, op: StdOp.call(lock, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(lockBytes)))
	module.appendOp(settled, op: StdOp.osLockInit(lock))
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: settled, label: SchedLockLabel, value: lock, ids: ids)

	// The `P*` array. `__slab_alloc` zeroes it, so a slot the loop has not reached yet reads as a null P
	// rather than as garbage — which matters only until the loop fills every one, and costs nothing.
	let arrayStride = emitConst(module, block: settled, value: PointerSlotBytes, ids: ids)
	let arrayBytes = emitBin(module, block: settled, a: numProcs, b: arrayStride, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let procs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(settled, op: StdOp.call(procs, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(arrayBytes)))
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: settled, label: SchedProcsLabel, value: procs, ids: ids)

	// The build loop. Its index is the header's phi — the on-the-fly SSA shape every runtime builder here
	// constructs directly, a runtime function having no parser.
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: settled, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let iterId = ids.mint()
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(iterId, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(settled, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	settled.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))

	// ⚠ The bound is `settled`'s block arg rather than a re-read of `__sched_num_procs`: `settled` dominates
	// this header, so the value is in scope, and reloading it every iteration would be the same fact read twice
	// per turn from a word only this function writes.
	let built = emitCmp(module, block: loopHdr, a: iterId, b: numProcs, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(built, thenBlockId: adopt.id, elseBlockId: loopBody.id))

	// P[i] = a fresh zeroed struct, plus the four fields a zero would be wrong for.
	let structBytes = emitConst(module, block: loopBody, value: PStructSize, ids: ids)
	let p = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.call(p, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(structBytes)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: loopBody, record: emitProcSlotAddr(module, block: loopBody, index: iterId, ids: ids), offset: 0, value: p)
	emitStoreField(module, block: loopBody, record: p, offset: POffId, value: iterId)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: loopBody, value: 1, ids: ids)
	// `rng = id + 1` — non-zero, because zero is a fixed point of xorshift. Reserved for the stealer.
	emitStoreField(module, block: loopBody, record: p, offset: POffRng, value: emitBin(module, block: loopBody, a: iterId, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids))
	let wakeEvent = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.osEventCreate(wakeEvent))
	emitStoreField(module, block: loopBody, record: p, offset: POffWakeEvent, value: wakeEvent)
	// ⭐ This P's OWN syscall stack. `osAllocPages` rather than `__slab_alloc` so the region is its own
	// PAGE-aligned commit — the top is then 16-aligned, which the shim relies on to land RSP 16-aligned at
	// the `call` (the global region this replaces carried the same requirement).
	let sysStackSize = emitConst(module, block: loopBody, value: GtSystemStackSize, ids: ids)
	let sysStackBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.osAllocPages(sysStackBase, sizeId: sysStackSize))
	emitStoreField(module, block: loopBody, record: p, offset: POffSystemStackSP, value: emitBin(module, block: loopBody, a: sysStackBase, b: sysStackSize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids))
	let nextIter = emitBin(module, block: loopBody, a: iterId, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopBody, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	loopBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextIter)))

	// Adopt P[0]: mark it active, point it at its own inline scheduler GT, and publish it in this OS
	// thread's TLS slot.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CLAIM HERE IS A PLAIN STORE AND NOT THE CAS THE SPAWN PATH USES, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS REAL
	// RATHER THAN AN INCONSISTENCY.** This runs once, on the main OS thread, before any other thread of this
	// process exists — there is nobody to contest it. The CAS exists because the spawn path runs with the
	// run-queue lock RELEASED and several Ms can reach it at once; see `emitSchedClaimProc`.
	let zeroIndex = emitConst(module, block: adopt, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let p0 = emitLoadProc(module, block: adopt, index: zeroIndex, ids: ids)
	emitSchedAdoptProc(module, block: adopt, p: p0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(adopt, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedInitProcs'

// ⭐ **THE THREE STORES THAT MAKE A P THIS OS THREAD'S — stated once, because P[0] and every worker M do
// exactly the same thing and a second copy is where they would come to disagree.** Mark the P active, point
// its `currentGt` at its own inline scheduler GT, and publish the P in this thread's TLS slot.
//
// The inline GT arrives zeroed from the slab, and almost every one of those zeros is correct: `stackBase ==
// 0` is what tells the syscall shim and the prologue guard that this context runs on a real OS-thread stack,
// `waiter == 0` is what tells `__gt_sleep` that it is a top-level driver, and both coroutine-queue ends are
// *"empty"*. Its TIB bounds are captured by the first `__gt_context_switch` away from it.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE ONE ZERO THAT IS NOT CORRECT IS `owner`, AND STAMPING IT IS THE FOURTH STORE (EC10).** This
// inline GT IS a green thread — the only kind there is until `spawn` — and **a green thread's owner is
// ITSELF**. That is what closes the recursion `GtRuntime.buildGtSpawn` opens by copying the spawner's owner:
// every coroutine, at any nesting depth, chases its owner chain to exactly one green thread, and this is the
// one place a green thread is born. A zero left here would be a green thread owned by nobody and the first
// `__gt_coro_enqueue` onto it would write through a null pointer.
//
// ⚠ It is stamped for EVERY P adopted, not only P[0]: a worker M's own scheduler context is a green thread
// on the same terms, so the day `spawn` starts one it is already its own owner and this function does not
// grow an arm.
function emitSchedAdoptProc(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let active = emitConst(module, block: block, value: PStatusActive, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffStatus, value: active)
	let mainThread = emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffMainThread, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffCurrentGt, value: mainThread)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: mainThread, offset: GtOffOwner, value: mainThread)
	let tlsIndex = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedTlsIndexLabel, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.tlsSetValue(tlsIndex, valueId: p))
end 'emitSchedAdoptProc'

// ⭐ **HOW MANY Ps TO BUILD:** `DefaultMaxProcs`, unless `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` names a larger positive integer,
// and never more than the machine reports. The answer reaches `settled` as its single block arg; every arm
// below terminates in a branch that supplies it.
//
// ⚠ **A MALFORMED OR ABSENT VALUE IS THE DEFAULT, NEVER AN ERROR.** `osEnvRead` answers 0 for unset, and the
// digit walk answers 0 for anything that does not start with a digit; both land on the same `< 1` test a
// literal `0` lands on, which is what makes *"leave the default alone"* one predicate rather than three.
function emitResolveMaxProcs(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, settled IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var lengthOk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocslen")
	var parseStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocsparse")
	var parsed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocsparsed")
	var clampToCpu = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocsclamp")
	var cpuFloor = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocscpufloor")
	var cpuKnown = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocscpuok")
	var takeCpu = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocscpu")
	var takeRequested = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocsreq")

	let nameBuf = emitLiteralCString(module, block: cur, ids: ids, text: MaxProcsEnvName)
	let bufBytes = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: MaxProcsEnvBufBytes, ids: ids)
	let buf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.call(buf, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(bufBytes)))
	let written = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.osEnvRead(written, namePtrId: nameBuf, bufPtrId: buf, bufLenId: bufBytes))

	// ⚠ **`osEnvRead` HAS THREE ANSWERS AND THIS SITE DECODES THEM DIFFERENTLY FROM ITS SIBLING, ON PURPOSE.**
	// `GetEnvironmentVariableA` returns 0 for unset, the CHARACTERS WRITTEN when the value fits, and the size it
	// NEEDS when the buffer is too small — and `SubprocessRuntime.emitSubpReadEnvInto` retries at exactly that
	// size, because a `PATH` legitimately overflows any guess. A processor count does not: a value longer than
	// 32 bytes is not one, so declining is the right answer and a retry would be machinery for a case that
	// cannot occur. The two are one API contract decoded twice, which is why they name each other here.
	//
	// A value that did not fit is not a processor count — decline rather than retry at the size Win32 named.
	let tooLong = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: written, b: bufBytes, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	let curDefault = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: DefaultMaxProcs, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(tooLong, thenBlockId: settled.id, elseBlockId: lengthOk.id))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(settled.id, argIds: argsOf(curDefault)))

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: lengthOk, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let unset = emitCmp(module, block: lengthOk, a: written, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let lengthOkDefault = emitConst(module, block: lengthOk, value: DefaultMaxProcs, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(lengthOk, op: StdOp.condBranch(unset, thenBlockId: settled.id, elseBlockId: parseStart.id))
	lengthOk.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(settled.id, argIds: argsOf(lengthOkDefault)))

	// The decimal walk hands its accumulator to `parsed` as that block's arg.
	let requested = ids.mint()
	parsed.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(requested, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitParseUnsignedDecimal(module, func: func, cur: parseStart, cstr: buf, parsed: parsed, ids: ids)

	let one = emitConst(module, block: parsed, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let belowOne = emitCmp(module, block: parsed, a: requested, b: one, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	let parsedDefault = emitConst(module, block: parsed, value: DefaultMaxProcs, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(parsed, op: StdOp.condBranch(belowOne, thenBlockId: settled.id, elseBlockId: clampToCpu.id))
	parsed.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(settled.id, argIds: argsOf(parsedDefault)))

	// ⭐ Never above the machine. `osCpuCount` is read directly rather than through `__cpu_count`, which is a
	// separate runtime entry a program opts into and which a scheduler must not drag in.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND ITS FLOOR IS APPLIED HERE, BECAUSE `osCpuCount` IS THE RAW ANSWER AND THE RAW ANSWER CAN BE
	// ZERO.** `GetActiveProcessorCount` returns 0 on failure and `sysconf` returns -1;
	// `CpuParallelRuntime.buildCpuCount` clamps for exactly that reason and has the sabotage to prove the arm
	// is reached. Excluding `requested < 1` one block up says nothing about `cpus`, and a `cpus` of 0 wins the
	// `requested > cpus` test below — so `numProcs` would be 0, `__slab_alloc(0)` would size the `P*` array,
	// the build loop would run zero times, and `adopt` would then read `__sched_procs[0]` out of a zero-byte
	// region and write three fields through whatever it found. A wild write, from a comment that said the
	// guard was unnecessary.
	let cpus = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(clampToCpu, op: StdOp.osCpuCount(cpus))
	let cpuFloorConst = emitConst(module, block: clampToCpu, value: MinimumProcessorCount, ids: ids)
	let belowFloor = emitCmp(module, block: clampToCpu, a: cpus, b: cpuFloorConst, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	let liveCpus = ids.mint()
	cpuKnown.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(liveCpus, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(clampToCpu, op: StdOp.condBranch(belowFloor, thenBlockId: cpuFloor.id, elseBlockId: cpuKnown.id))
	clampToCpu.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(cpuKnown.id, argIds: argsOf(cpus)))
	let flooredCpus = emitConst(module, block: cpuFloor, value: MinimumProcessorCount, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cpuFloor, op: StdOp.branch(cpuKnown.id))
	cpuFloor.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(cpuKnown.id, argIds: argsOf(flooredCpus)))

	let overCpu = emitCmp(module, block: cpuKnown, a: requested, b: liveCpus, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cpuKnown, op: StdOp.condBranch(overCpu, thenBlockId: takeCpu.id, elseBlockId: takeRequested.id))

	module.setTerminator(takeCpu, op: StdOp.branch(settled.id))
	takeCpu.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(settled.id, argIds: argsOf(liveCpus)))
	module.setTerminator(takeRequested, op: StdOp.branch(settled.id))
	takeRequested.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(settled.id, argIds: argsOf(requested)))
end 'emitResolveMaxProcs'

// The unsigned decimal value of the NUL-terminated bytes at `cstr`, delivered as `parsed`'s block arg.
// Ports the bootstrap's `EmitParseUnsignedCstrIntoRax`: the walk stops at the first non-digit (the
// terminator included), so a trailing suffix is IGNORED rather than rejected, and a string that does not
// start with a digit answers 0 — which every caller reads as *"leave the default alone"*.
function emitParseUnsignedDecimal(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, cstr ValueId, parsed IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var scanHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"decscan")
	var notBelow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"decnotbelow")
	var scanStep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"decstep")

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let indexId = ids.mint()
	let accId = ids.mint()
	scanHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(indexId, argType: StdType.i64))
	scanHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(accId, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(scanHdr.id))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(zero, second: zero)))

	// The digit band is tested at BOTH ends rather than by one unsigned wrap: the byte load zero-extends, so
	// the wrap trick would work — and it would also rest on the load's width, which is exactly the kind of
	// implicit dependency a later widening breaks silently. Two compares, no premise.
	let ch = emitLoadByteIndexed(module, block: scanHdr, base: cstr, index: indexId, ids: ids)
	let asciiZero = emitConst(module, block: scanHdr, value: AsciiZeroDigit, ids: ids)
	let below = emitCmp(module, block: scanHdr, a: ch, b: asciiZero, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(scanHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(below, thenBlockId: parsed.id, elseBlockId: notBelow.id))
	scanHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(parsed.id, argIds: argsOf(accId)))

	let asciiNine = emitConst(module, block: notBelow, value: AsciiNineDigit, ids: ids)
	let above = emitCmp(module, block: notBelow, a: ch, b: asciiNine, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(notBelow, op: StdOp.condBranch(above, thenBlockId: parsed.id, elseBlockId: scanStep.id))
	notBelow.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(parsed.id, argIds: argsOf(accId)))

	let stepAsciiZero = emitConst(module, block: scanStep, value: AsciiZeroDigit, ids: ids)
	let digit = emitBin(module, block: scanStep, a: ch, b: stepAsciiZero, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let radix = emitConst(module, block: scanStep, value: DecimalRadix, ids: ids)
	let scaled = emitBin(module, block: scanStep, a: accId, b: radix, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let nextAcc = emitBin(module, block: scanStep, a: scaled, b: digit, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: scanStep, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextIndex = emitBin(module, block: scanStep, a: indexId, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(scanStep, op: StdOp.branch(scanHdr.id))
	scanStep.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(nextIndex, second: nextAcc)))
end 'emitParseUnsignedDecimal'

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_drain_pending`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__sched_drain_pending()` — THE OTHER HALF OF EVERY DEFERRAL, AND THE REASON THERE ARE THREE
// SLOTS.** It runs on this P's scheduler context, AFTER the context switch that made deferral necessary, and
// acts on whatever the three slots hold. What they have in common is the WINDOW they close: between the
// moment a green thread hands over its note and the moment `__gt_context_switch` has finished saving its
// registers, that thread must not be discoverable by anybody — not by an M that could pop it, and not by a
// timer check on another M that could fire it. Here, control has come back through the switch, so the save
// is complete.
//
//   • **`POffPendingPark` — a thread that is about to WAIT (W218).** `__gt_register_park` puts it on the
//     timer heap, the process table or the IOCP handshake, and stores `waiting` in the same breath.
//   • **`POffPendingWaiter` — a woken thread**, published as a WAKEUP (`__sched_publish`).
//   • **`POffPendingYielder` — a thread that gave up its turn**, published as a YIELD
//     (`__sched_publish_yield`), which for a green thread is the GLOBAL queue's tail rather than this P's
//     ring. `GtLayout.cs:388-402` records what routing a yielder to the front cost the bootstrap: *"a
//     thousand yields from one green thread left a sibling that had never run still unrun"*.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE PARK SLOT IS DRAINED FIRST, AND THAT ORDER IS A DEADLOCK ARGUMENT RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.**
// An IO park's registration is two PLAIN stores and takes no lock, because the IOCP completion thread
// spin-waits on `gt.ioParked` *while holding `__sched_lock`* (`X64GtRuntime`'s completion loop). Every other
// arm here TAKES that lock. So a drain that published a waiter or a yielder first would block on
// `EnterCriticalSection` behind a completion thread that is spinning for the very store this drain has not
// reached yet — a hang, with no diagnostic. Draining the park first makes that unrepresentable: by the time
// anything here wants the lock, `ioParked` is published and the completion thread can finish.
//
// ⚠ **THE WAITER SLOT STILL HAS NO PRODUCER, AND ITS ABSENCE IS DELIBERATE RATHER THAN UNFINISHED.** The
// reference writes it from the netpoll's completion claim; shv2's completion thread solves the same problem
// with the `ioParked` handshake instead, so there is no site here that needs it today. It is drained anyway,
// which costs one load and one compare, so the day a producer appears it is a store and nothing else.
function buildSchedDrainPending(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedDrainPendingName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var registerPark = module.addBlock(func, label: b"drainpark")
	var waiterTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"drainwaiter")
	var yielderTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"drainyielder")
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	let p = emitLoadCurrentP(module, block: entry, ids: ids)

	// The park slot's action is a REGISTRATION rather than a publish, so it does not go through
	// `emitSchedDrainSlot` — but the clear-before-acting rule is the same one, for the same reason.
	let parked = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: p, offset: POffPendingPark, ids: ids)
	let parkZero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let noPark = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: parked, b: parkZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(noPark, thenBlockId: waiterTest.id, elseBlockId: registerPark.id))
	emitStoreField(module, block: registerPark, record: p, offset: POffPendingPark, value: parkZero)
	module.appendOp(registerPark, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtRegisterParkName, args: argsOf(parked)))
	module.setTerminator(registerPark, op: StdOp.branch(waiterTest.id))

	emitSchedDrainSlot(module, func: func, cur: waiterTest, p: p, offset: POffPendingWaiter, publishCallee: SchedPublishName, cont: yielderTest, ids: ids)
	emitSchedDrainSlot(module, func: func, cur: yielderTest, p: p, offset: POffPendingYielder, publishCallee: SchedPublishYieldName, cont: doneBlock, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedDrainPending'

// One slot's drain: if it holds a GT, clear it and publish it through `publishCallee`; either way continue at
// `cont`. Stated once because the two slots differ only in their offset and in WHICH publish they are — a
// wakeup or a yield, which for a green thread is a different end of the hierarchy and for a coroutine is the
// same tail.
//
// ⚠ Neither slot takes the run-queue lock HERE: both publish doors take it themselves on the arm that needs
// it. This function is called from a scheduler loop rather than from a site that already holds it, so that is
// safe — and the park arm above, which runs before either, is why it stays safe.
function emitSchedDrainSlot(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, p ValueId, offset ByteOffset, publishCallee ByteArray, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var enqueue = module.addBlock(func, label: b"drainpush")

	let pending = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: p, offset: offset, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let empty = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: pending, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(empty, thenBlockId: cont.id, elseBlockId: enqueue.id))

	// Clear BEFORE the enqueue: once the GT is discoverable another M may park a fresh deferral in this slot,
	// and a clear afterwards would erase it.
	let clear = emitConst(module, block: enqueue, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: enqueue, record: p, offset: offset, value: clear)
	module.appendOp(enqueue, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: publishCallee, args: argsOf(pending)))
	module.setTerminator(enqueue, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitSchedDrainSlot'

// ==========================================================================
// The per-P ring — `__sched_runq_put` / `__sched_runq_get` / `__sched_steal`
// ==========================================================================

// The byte address of the ring slot `counter` names — `p + POffRunqBase + (counter and 255) * 8`. Stated
// once because five sites index the ring and the modulus is the whole reason the counters may be monotonic;
// a site that spelled the mask itself would be the one that later addressed a slot the others do not.
function emitRunqSlotAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, counter ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let mask = emitConst(module, block: block, value: RunqIndexMask, ids: ids)
	let index = emitBin(module, block: block, a: counter, b: mask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	return emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: block, base: emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffRunqBase, ids: ids), index: index, ids: ids)
end 'emitRunqSlotAddr'

// ⭐⭐ **PUBLISH THE RING TAIL — AND THIS ONE INSTRUCTION IS THE DEKKER PAIR'S FENCE.** It advances
// `p.runqTail` by `delta` with a `lock xadd` where a plain store would do, because on x86 a plain store may
// be reordered past a LATER LOAD — and the later load is `__sched_wake_or_spawn`'s scan of every P's
// `idleFlag`. Until W212 the fence between the publish and that scan was the run-queue LOCK RELEASE, which
// a lockless ring push does not have; `buildSchedWakeOrSpawn` states the pairing in full and this is the
// other half of it. It is also the store-release that makes the slot visible to a thief.
//
// ⛔ **DO NOT "OPTIMISE" IT INTO A PLAIN STORE.** The tail has exactly one writer, so the atomicity buys
// nothing and the BARRIER buys everything; the failure it prevents is a green thread sitting in a ring while
// the M that could run it sleeps out its park timeout — latency, not a fault, which is why it would survive
// a green suite. One locked op per push, a class G2 measured at ~25 cycles.
function emitRunqPublishTail(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, delta ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let tailAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.atomicRmw(ids.mint(), addrId: tailAddr, operandId: delta, opcode: StdAtomicRmwOpcode.add))
end 'emitRunqPublishTail'

// `1` if this CAS lost. Both ring CASes and the grab's read it the same way, and `AtomicCasFailed` is the
// contract's own spelling of the answer (`StdDialect`), not a literal 0 restated three times.
function emitCasLost(module StdModule, block IrBlock, result ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let failed = emitConst(module, block: block, value: AtomicCasFailed, ids: ids)
	return emitCmp(module, block: block, a: result, b: failed, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
end 'emitCasLost'

// ⭐⭐ **DO THE SCHEDULER'S TIERS HOLD A GREEN THREAD RIGHT NOW? — the yield's PEEK, and the door W212 had,
// EC10 deleted and SV1 needs back.** `POffRunqHead` predicted this exact rung: *"a `spawn` rung that needs
// it back"*. `__gt_resched`'s hand-off arm gives this processor up to a DRIVER, and the driver looks here
// (`__sched_find_runnable`); asking first is what keeps a yield with nobody to yield to on the POLL arm,
// where the due timers and the exited children are.
//
// ⚠ **GO RE-READS THE COUNTERS AND THIS DOES NOT — because shv2 has no `runnext`.** Go's `runqempty` loops
// because a `runnext` slot can hold a thread while head and tail agree; `__sched_runq_get` is built *"minus
// the `runnext` slot shv2 deliberately does not build"* (`POffRunnext` names the rung that would build one,
// and it is the rung that would have to restore the loop). Until then `tail != head` IS a non-empty ring,
// and one read of each is exact rather than optimistic.
//
// ⚠ **IT DOES NOT WALK THE OTHER Ps' RINGS, AND THAT IS ARGUED RATHER THAN FORGOTTEN.** Work sitting on
// another P is already owned by an M that is running it, so a yield that polled instead has starved nobody
// — while a peek at every P would put a scan on the yield fast path to sharpen an answer neither arm needs
// sharp. Both loads are unlocked for `__gt_resched`'s own stated reason: they choose an ARM, and both arms
// are correct whatever they answer.
export function emitSchedAnyRunnable(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let tail = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	let ringHasWork = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: head, b: tail, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	let globalHead = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: GtRunQueueHeadLabel, ids: ids)
	let noGlobalHead = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let globalHasWork = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: globalHead, b: noGlobalHead, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)

	// Both operands are `cmp` results, so they are 0 or 1 and a bitwise OR is the logical one — the argument
	// `emitGtIsDropped` makes for its own `and`.
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: ringHasWork, b: globalHasWork, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedAnyRunnable'

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_runq_put(gt)` — PUSH A RUNNABLE GREEN THREAD ONTO THE CURRENT P's RING, WITH NO LOCK.**
// Go's `runqput(pp, gp, next: false)` (`proc.go:7529`) plus `runqputslow` (`proc.go:7576`), which is where a
// full ring goes.
//
// ⚠⚠ **ONLY THE OWNING P's M MAY PUSH A RING, which is what makes the tail a single-writer word and the
// push three instructions — AND THE RULE IS NOW ENFORCED HERE RATHER THAN OWED BY EVERY CALLER (SV1).** The
// IOCP completion thread owns no P: `emitLoadCurrentP` answers 0 for it, and a ring push through a null P
// would store `gt` at offset `POffRunqBase` of address 0. It publishes GREEN THREADS now (a parked reader
// resumed by a completion packet may be one), so the `p == 0` arm below routes it to the GLOBAL queue — the
// tier that is everybody's and is behind a lock — and no caller has to remember which kind of thread it is
// on.
//
// **THE OVERFLOW IS A ROUTING DECISION, NEVER AN ERROR.** A full ring moves its OLDEST HALF plus the new
// thread to the global queue, so the ring keeps the freshest work and the rest becomes everybody's. The
// 61-schedule fairness check in `__sched_find_runnable` is what stops the half that moved from starving.
//
// ⭐ **THE BATCH IS READ *AFTER* THE HEAD CAS, WHERE GO READS IT BEFORE — AND THE REASON IS THAT GO NEEDS A
// SCRATCH ARRAY AND SHV2 DOES NOT.** `runqputslow` copies 128 entries into a frame array, CASes the head and
// then links them, and the copy has to come first because its own linking overwrites each G's `schedlink`.
// Here the batch goes onto the global FIFO one at a time, so there is nothing to stage — and after a WON CAS
// the slots `[h, h+n)` belong to this thread alone: no thief can claim them (the head has moved past) and no
// push can overwrite them, because this function is the ring's only pusher and it has not pushed yet. A 1 KB
// frame array avoided, and one fewer place for two copies of a batch to disagree.
function buildSchedRunqPut(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedRunqPutName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var globalOnly = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqnoproc")
	var retry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqretry")
	var push = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqpush")
	var claim = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqclaim")
	var moveLock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqmovelock")
	var moveHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqmovehdr")
	var moveBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqmovebody")
	var moveNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqmovenext")
	var moveDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqmovedone")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let gt = 0 as ValueId

	let p = emitLoadCurrentP(module, block: entry, ids: ids)
	let entryZero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let noProc = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: p, b: entryZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(noProc, thenBlockId: globalOnly.id, elseBlockId: retry.id))

	// globalOnly: this OS thread owns no P, so it has no ring to push — the global queue is where a thread
	// belongs when nobody owns it yet, and it is exactly what `__sched_find_runnable`'s batch grab is for. The
	// lock is taken HERE rather than owed by the caller: a CRITICAL_SECTION is recursive, so the one caller
	// that already holds it (the IOCP completion loop, through `__sched_publish_locked`) pays two counted ops
	// and nothing else — against a precondition no site could check.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: globalOnly, ids: ids)
	emitSchedEnqueueLocked(module, block: globalOnly, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: globalOnly, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(globalOnly, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// retry: read both counters and ask whether the ring is full. `tail - head` IS the length — see
	// `POffRunqHead` for why no third word says so. A plain load of the head is a load-ACQUIRE on the one
	// lane that has green threads (x64 never reorders load/load), which is the ordering Go spells out.
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: retry, record: p, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let tail = emitLoadField(module, block: retry, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	let queued = emitBin(module, block: retry, a: tail, b: head, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let capacity = emitConst(module, block: retry, value: RunqSlots, ids: ids)
	let full = emitCmp(module, block: retry, a: queued, b: capacity, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(retry, op: StdOp.condBranch(full, thenBlockId: claim.id, elseBlockId: push.id))

	// push: write the slot, THEN publish the tail. The order is the whole contract with a thief — the locked
	// tail bump is a release, so a thief that sees the new tail sees the slot that tail describes.
	emitStoreField(module, block: push, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: push, p: p, counter: tail, ids: ids), offset: 0, value: gt)
	let oneSlot = emitConst(module, block: push, value: 1, ids: ids)
	emitRunqPublishTail(module, block: push, p: p, delta: oneSlot, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(push, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// claim: take the oldest half by CAS. A thief that beat us to it leaves the ring no longer full, so the
	// lost CAS re-reads and the push above succeeds — Go's `goto retry`, and the reason this is a loop.
	let batchCount = emitConst(module, block: claim, value: RunqHalfSlots, ids: ids)
	let claimedHead = emitBin(module, block: claim, a: head, b: batchCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let headAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: claim, record: p, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let claimed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(claim, op: StdOp.atomicCas(claimed, addrId: headAddr, expectedId: head, replacementId: claimedHead))
	let lostClaim = emitCasLost(module, block: claim, result: claimed, ids: ids)
	let moveIndex = ids.mint()
	moveHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(moveIndex, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(claim, op: StdOp.condBranch(lostClaim, thenBlockId: retry.id, elseBlockId: moveLock.id))

	// ⛔⛔ **THE ACQUIRE GETS A BLOCK OF ITS OWN, AND BOTH NEIGHBOURS WOULD BE A HANG.** `osLockEnter` is a
	// Win32 CRITICAL_SECTION, so it COUNTS: an acquire in the loop header below would recurse it 128 times
	// against the one release at `moveDone`, and an acquire in `claim` would be taken again on every lost-CAS
	// retry. Both leave the lock held for the process's life, and neither is a fault — the failure is a hang in
	// the next thread to want the global queue. One acquire, on the one edge that runs exactly once.
	//
	// ⚠ **A CALLER THAT ALREADY HOLDS THE LOCK REACHES HERE, AND THAT IS SAFE**, because the same recursion
	// counts a nested pair correctly. What such a caller must not rely on is this leave being the StoreLoad
	// fence for its own wake — a recursive leave releases nothing — and none does: the ring's fence is
	// `emitRunqPublishTail`'s locked op, and a batch publisher's is its own outer release.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: moveLock, ids: ids)
	let moveStart = emitConst(module, block: moveLock, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(moveLock, op: StdOp.branch(moveHdr.id))
	moveLock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(moveHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(moveStart)))

	let moved = emitCmp(module, block: moveHdr, a: moveIndex, b: batchCount, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(moveHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(moved, thenBlockId: moveDone.id, elseBlockId: moveBody.id))

	// ⛔⛔ **A TOMBSTONE IN THE BATCH MOVES LIKE A LIVE THREAD, AND THAT IS THE FIX RATHER THAN A
	// SIMPLIFICATION (SV1).** This loop used to ask `emitGtIsDropped` of every slot and call
	// `__gt_runner_done` on the ones that answered — **while holding `__sched_lock`**, which is the one thing
	// that call may never be made under: it reaches `__gt_reclaim`'s releaser, and a releaser that reaches
	// `__gt_promise_drop` spin-waits with a release that a recursive CRITICAL_SECTION turns into a no-op. See
	// `GtRuntime.GtRunnerDoneName`, where the rule is written down.
	//
	// ⚠ **NOTHING IS LEAKED BY MOVING ONE.** A tombstone on the global queue is refused and reclaimed by
	// whoever pops it — `emitGtTombstoneHandback`, reached from `__sched_find_runnable` with the lock RELEASED,
	// which is the same reclaim on the correct side of the lock. What the drop itself sweeps
	// (`__sched_sweep_dropped`) is the FRONT of this ring and of the global queue, so the bound
	// `async-promise-drop.spawn-drop-loop-bounded` pins is unchanged.
	let slotCounter = emitBin(module, block: moveBody, a: head, b: moveIndex, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let movingGt = emitLoadField(module, block: moveBody, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: moveBody, p: p, counter: slotCounter, ids: ids), offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitSchedEnqueueLocked(module, block: moveBody, gt: movingGt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(moveBody, op: StdOp.branch(moveNext.id))

	let moveStep = emitConst(module, block: moveNext, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextMoveIndex = emitBin(module, block: moveNext, a: moveIndex, b: moveStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(moveNext, op: StdOp.branch(moveHdr.id))
	moveNext.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(moveHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextMoveIndex)))

	// The new thread goes to the global tail BEHIND the batch, which is Go's order (`batch[n] = gp`) and the
	// only one that keeps the ring's FIFO promise across an overflow.
	emitSchedEnqueueLocked(module, block: moveDone, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: moveDone, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(moveDone, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedRunqPut'

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_runq_get(p) -> gt` — TAKE THE OLDEST THREAD OFF `p`'s RING, OR 0.** Go's `runqget`
// (`proc.go:7650`), minus the `runnext` slot shv2 deliberately does not build (`POffRunnext`).
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE CAS ON THE HEAD IS WHAT MAKES A DOUBLE POP STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE, AND THAT IS THIS RUNG'S
// WHOLE THESIS.** The bootstrap puts each P's LOCAL queue under the GLOBAL lock and records why
// (`RuntimeEmitter.Scheduler.cs:262-267`): without it a thief and the owner both popped the same head node —
// *"kind=local and kind=steal_first on the same gt within the same millisecond, immediately followed by
// SIGSEGV"*. Here the two contend on ONE word with a compare-and-swap, so exactly one of them can advance
// past a given index and the loser re-reads. A plain load/store here IS that SIGSEGV.
//
// ⚠ **THE SLOT IS READ BEFORE THE CAS**, which looks like the wrong order and is Go's. A read of a slot the
// CAS then fails to claim is harmless — the value is discarded — while reading it afterwards would be reading
// a slot the winner may already have overwritten.
function buildSchedRunqGet(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedRunqGetName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgloop")
	var take = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgtake")
	var took = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgtook")
	var emptyBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgempty")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let p = 0 as ValueId
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))

	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: loopHdr, record: p, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let tail = emitLoadField(module, block: loopHdr, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	let empty = emitCmp(module, block: loopHdr, a: head, b: tail, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(empty, thenBlockId: emptyBlock.id, elseBlockId: take.id))

	let g = emitLoadField(module, block: take, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: take, p: p, counter: head, ids: ids), offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let headAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: take, record: p, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let oneSlot = emitConst(module, block: take, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextHead = emitBin(module, block: take, a: head, b: oneSlot, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let won = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(take, op: StdOp.atomicCas(won, addrId: headAddr, expectedId: head, replacementId: nextHead))
	let lost = emitCasLost(module, block: take, result: won, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(take, op: StdOp.condBranch(lost, thenBlockId: loopHdr.id, elseBlockId: took.id))

	// `take` is `took`'s only predecessor, so `g` is in scope with no block argument — the dominance argument
	// every branching emitter in this runtime makes for itself.
	module.setTerminator(took, op: StdOp.ret(g))
	emitConstReturn(module, block: emptyBlock, value: 0, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedRunqGet'

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_steal(p, victim) -> gt` — GRAB HALF OF `victim`'s RING INTO `p`'s, AND ANSWER ONE OF THEM.**
// Go's `runqgrab` (`proc.go:7714`) and `runqsteal` (`proc.go:7782`) as ONE function, because Go's split
// exists to serve a second caller (`runqdrain`) that shv2 has no equivalent of.
//
// **HALF, ROUNDED UP** (`n - n/2`), so a victim holding ONE thread still gives it up — which is what makes a
// pair of processors converge instead of one of them spinning on a queue it is never allowed to empty.
//
// ⚠ **THE `n > half` RE-READ IS NOT DEFENSIVE, it is the one place an inconsistent pair of counters can be
// observed.** Head and tail are read separately while the victim's owner is pushing between them, so a tail
// from before a wrap paired with a head from after it computes a length larger than the ring exists to hold.
// Go re-reads for exactly this, and says so at the same line.
//
// ⛔ **ITS PRECONDITION IS THAT `p`'s RING IS EMPTY, and unlike Go's this one cannot be violated.** Go's
// `runqsteal` carries a `throw("runqsteal: runq overflow")` because its `stealWork` may RUN TIMERS first,
// which readies an arbitrary number of Gs onto the thief's OWN ring before the grab (`proc.go:3886-3893`
// says so outright). shv2's stealing phase runs no timers, is reached only after `__sched_runq_get` answered
// 0, and the ring's only pusher is the very thread running this — so the free space is the whole ring and
// the grab is at most half of one.
function buildSchedSteal(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedStealName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var grabHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sgrab")
	var sized = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ssized")
	var copyHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scopyhdr")
	var copyBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scopybody")
	var commit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scommit")
	var publish = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spublish")
	var publishTail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spubtail")
	var answer = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sanswer")
	var failBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sfail")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let p = 0 as ValueId
	let victim = 1 as ValueId

	// Our own tail, read once: nobody else writes it, and the copy addresses slots relative to it.
	let ourTail = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(grabHdr.id))

	let vHead = emitLoadField(module, block: grabHdr, record: victim, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let vTail = emitLoadField(module, block: grabHdr, record: victim, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	let vLen = emitBin(module, block: grabHdr, a: vTail, b: vHead, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let halfShift = emitConst(module, block: grabHdr, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let vHalf = emitBin(module, block: grabHdr, a: vLen, b: halfShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	let grabCount = emitBin(module, block: grabHdr, a: vLen, b: vHalf, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let grabZero = emitConst(module, block: grabHdr, value: 0, ids: ids)
	// `<= 0` and not `== 0`: a torn read can make `vLen` negative, and a negative count would run the copy
	// loop zero times and then answer a slot nobody wrote. Both bad readings land on the same arm.
	let nothing = emitCmp(module, block: grabHdr, a: grabCount, b: grabZero, pred: StdCmpPred.le, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(grabHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothing, thenBlockId: failBlock.id, elseBlockId: sized.id))

	let grabCap = emitConst(module, block: sized, value: RunqHalfSlots, ids: ids)
	let inconsistent = emitCmp(module, block: sized, a: grabCount, b: grabCap, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	let copyStart = emitConst(module, block: sized, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let copyIndex = ids.mint()
	copyHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(copyIndex, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(sized, op: StdOp.condBranch(inconsistent, thenBlockId: grabHdr.id, elseBlockId: copyHdr.id))
	sized.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(copyStart)))

	let copied = emitCmp(module, block: copyHdr, a: copyIndex, b: grabCount, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(copyHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(copied, thenBlockId: commit.id, elseBlockId: copyBody.id))

	let fromCounter = emitBin(module, block: copyBody, a: vHead, b: copyIndex, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let stolenGt = emitLoadField(module, block: copyBody, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: copyBody, p: victim, counter: fromCounter, ids: ids), offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let toCounter = emitBin(module, block: copyBody, a: ourTail, b: copyIndex, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: copyBody, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: copyBody, p: p, counter: toCounter, ids: ids), offset: 0, value: stolenGt)
	let copyStep = emitConst(module, block: copyBody, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextCopyIndex = emitBin(module, block: copyBody, a: copyIndex, b: copyStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(copyBody, op: StdOp.branch(copyHdr.id))
	copyBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextCopyIndex)))

	// commit: the copy is only a copy until the victim's head moves past it. A lost CAS means the victim (or
	// another thief) consumed some of what we read, so everything above is discarded and re-read.
	let vHeadAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: commit, record: victim, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let newVHead = emitBin(module, block: commit, a: vHead, b: grabCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let grabbed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(commit, op: StdOp.atomicCas(grabbed, addrId: vHeadAddr, expectedId: vHead, replacementId: newVHead))
	let lostGrab = emitCasLost(module, block: commit, result: grabbed, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(commit, op: StdOp.condBranch(lostGrab, thenBlockId: grabHdr.id, elseBlockId: publish.id))

	// publish: the LAST of the grabbed threads is the answer and is NOT published — this M runs it now — so
	// the tail advances by one fewer than we took. Taking the last rather than the first leaves the ones that
	// stay in the ring in their original order, which is Go's choice and the FIFO promise's.
	let publishStep = emitConst(module, block: publish, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let keptCount = emitBin(module, block: publish, a: grabCount, b: publishStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let answerCounter = emitBin(module, block: publish, a: ourTail, b: keptCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let answerGt = emitLoadField(module, block: publish, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: publish, p: p, counter: answerCounter, ids: ids), offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let publishZero = emitConst(module, block: publish, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let nonePublished = emitCmp(module, block: publish, a: keptCount, b: publishZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(publish, op: StdOp.condBranch(nonePublished, thenBlockId: answer.id, elseBlockId: publishTail.id))

	emitRunqPublishTail(module, block: publishTail, p: p, delta: keptCount, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(publishTail, op: StdOp.branch(answer.id))

	module.setTerminator(answer, op: StdOp.ret(answerGt))
	emitConstReturn(module, block: failBlock, value: 0, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedSteal'

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_find_runnable`
// ==========================================================================

// Advance `p.rng` one xorshift64 step and answer the new value — `x ^= x<<13; x ^= x>>7; x ^= x<<17`, the
// generator both references use for victim selection (v1's `arm64GtXorshift`,
// `Arm64MacosGreenThread.maxon:1233`; the bootstrap's `EmitXorshift64`).
//
// ⚠ **THE SHIFT RIGHT IS LOGICAL, AND THE ARITHMETIC ONE WOULD BREAK IT.** xorshift64 is defined over
// unsigned words; an arithmetic shift smears the sign bit and the sequence degenerates. The value is then
// consumed by an UNSIGNED modulus for the same reason: a signed `mod` of a negative draw would index the P
// array below zero.
//
// ⚠ **THE RESULT MUST BE WRITTEN BACK** or the "generator" answers one constant for ever — v1 says exactly
// this at its own copy, which is the kind of remark that only gets written after it has happened once.
function emitXorshiftStep(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let seed = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffRng, ids: ids)
	let up13 = emitBin(module, block: block, a: seed, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 13, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let mixed13 = emitBin(module, block: block, a: seed, b: up13, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitXor, ids: ids)
	let down7 = emitBin(module, block: block, a: mixed13, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 7, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	let mixed7 = emitBin(module, block: block, a: mixed13, b: down7, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitXor, ids: ids)
	let up17 = emitBin(module, block: block, a: mixed7, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 17, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let stepped = emitBin(module, block: block, a: mixed7, b: up17, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitXor, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffRng, value: stepped)
	return stepped
end 'emitXorshiftStep'

// `a mod b` where both are known non-negative — the P-index arithmetic, and the ONE place the signedness of
// that modulus is decided. See `emitXorshiftStep` for why a signed one would be a wild index.
function emitUnsignedMod(module StdModule, block IrBlock, a ValueId, b ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let v = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.mod(v, lhs: a, rhs: b, signedness: DivideSignedness.unsigned))
	return v
end 'emitUnsignedMod'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__sched_find_runnable() -> gt` — THE ONE PLACE THE SCHEDULER DECIDES WHAT RUNS NEXT, and 0 when
// there is nothing to run.** Go's `findRunnable` (`proc.go:3405`) in Go's ORDER, with everything shv2 has no
// equivalent of removed (the trace reader, the GC workers, netpoll's optimistic poll, the spinning-M
// accounting, timer stealing):
//
//   1. **every 61st schedule, the GLOBAL queue first** — otherwise a ring that keeps refilling starves it;
//   2. **this P's own ring** (`__sched_runq_get`, a CAS on the head);
//   3. **a BATCH off the global queue** into this ring, so one lock acquisition serves many schedules;
//   4. **four rounds of STEALING**, each visiting every other active P once from a random start.
//
// ⭐ **IT REPLACED THREE HAND COPIES OF `lock; __gt_dequeue; unlock`** — `__gt_drive_until`'s drive, the
// worker loop's dequeue and its park re-dequeue, plus `__gt_resched`'s GT0 arm — and the bootstrap says why
// one door matters at exactly this point (`RuntimeEmitter.Scheduler.cs:257`): *"THIS IS THE ONE PLACE THE
// SCHEDULER DECIDES WHAT RUNS NEXT"*, which is what makes a policy (fairness, stealing, the dropped-thread
// refusal) a property of the scheduler rather than of whichever caller remembered it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS WHERE A DROPPED THREAD IS REFUSED.** A ring cannot be spliced, so `__gt_promise_drop`
// renounces a never-run thread and leaves it queued as a tombstone (`GtRuntime.GtOffTeardown`); the candidate
// this function is about to hand back is therefore checked, reclaimed if it is one, and the selection re-run.
// Doing it here rather than in each driver is what makes "a dropped thread never runs" true of every path at
// once — including a thread that reached this P inside a STOLEN batch, which no drop could have reached.
//
// ⚠ **THE CHECK IS NOT A CLAIM, AND IT DOES NOT NEED TO BE.** The POP is the claim: the ring's head CAS and
// the global queue's lock each hand a given thread to exactly one taker, so the party that reads the
// tombstone here is the only party that can. A promise renounced AFTER this read simply leaves a thread that
// runs to completion and is reclaimed by its driver's hand-off — the rendezvous makes the late race a wasted
// turn rather than a leak, which is the property that let this stay a plain load.
//
// ⚠ **THE VICTIM WALK IS A RANDOM START PLUS A FULL SEQUENTIAL SWEEP, WHERE GO USES A COPRIME STRIDE**
// (`stealOrder.start(cheaprand())`, an `randomEnum` over a coprime step). Both visit every P exactly once
// per round; the stride exists to decorrelate the ORDER in which many thieves walk the same array, which
// matters at Go's tens-to-hundreds of Ps and buys nothing at the handful shv2 runs — while a coprime table
// is a second structure to build at init and to size. The random START is kept, because that is what stops
// every idle P from hammering P[0].
function buildSchedFindRunnable(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedFindRunnableName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var fairPeek = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frfairpeek")
	var fairTake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frfairtake")
	var localPop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frlocal")
	var globalPeek = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frglobalpeek")
	var batchStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatch")
	var batchHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatchhdr")
	var batchTake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatchtake")
	var batchPublish = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatchpublish")
	var batchTail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatchtail")
	var batchUnlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatchunlock")
	var roundHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frroundhdr")
	var roundNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frroundnext")
	var victimHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frvictimhdr")
	var victimTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frvictimtest")
	var victimActive = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frvictimactive")
	var victimTry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frvictimtry")
	var victimNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frvictimnext")
	var stole = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frstole")
	var candidate = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frcandidate")

	// The candidate merge — every source of a green thread reaches it, and it is the only exit.
	let found = ids.mint()
	candidate.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(found, argType: StdType.i64))

	let p = emitLoadCurrentP(module, block: entry, ids: ids)

	// The schedule counter. Bumped once per CALL rather than once per thread handed back, so the retry a
	// reclaimed thread causes does not spend a second tick — a dropped thread was never a schedule.
	let tickStep = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let tick = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: p, offset: POffSchedTick, ids: ids), b: tickStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: p, offset: POffSchedTick, value: tick)
	let interval = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: GtFairnessInterval, ids: ids)
	let phase = emitUnsignedMod(module, block: entry, a: tick, b: interval, ids: ids)
	let entryZero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let atFairness = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: phase, b: entryZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(atFairness, thenBlockId: fairPeek.id, elseBlockId: localPop.id))

	// The unlocked peek before the lock — Go's `!sched.runq.empty()` guard, and safe for the same reason
	// `__gt_resched`'s arm choice is: it decides only whether to LOOK, and the locked dequeue behind it is
	// what decides anything.
	let fairHead = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: fairPeek, label: GtRunQueueHeadLabel, ids: ids)
	let fairZero = emitConst(module, block: fairPeek, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let fairEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: fairPeek, a: fairHead, b: fairZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(fairPeek, op: StdOp.condBranch(fairEmpty, thenBlockId: localPop.id, elseBlockId: fairTake.id))

	let fairGt = emitSchedDequeueLocked(module, block: fairTake, ids: ids)
	let fairTakeZero = emitConst(module, block: fairTake, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let fairMissed = emitCmp(module, block: fairTake, a: fairGt, b: fairTakeZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(fairTake, op: StdOp.condBranch(fairMissed, thenBlockId: localPop.id, elseBlockId: candidate.id))
	fairTake.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(candidate.id, argIds: argsOf(fairGt)))

	// localPop is also the RETRY target after a reclaimed thread: the fairness tick has already been spent,
	// and the global queue has already been consulted on the arm that led here.
	let localGt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(localPop, op: StdOp.call(localGt, callee: SchedRunqGetName, args: argsOf(p)))
	let localZero = emitConst(module, block: localPop, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let localEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: localPop, a: localGt, b: localZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(localPop, op: StdOp.condBranch(localEmpty, thenBlockId: globalPeek.id, elseBlockId: candidate.id))
	localPop.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(candidate.id, argIds: argsOf(localGt)))

	let batchHead = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: globalPeek, label: GtRunQueueHeadLabel, ids: ids)
	let peekZero = emitConst(module, block: globalPeek, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let globalEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: globalPeek, a: batchHead, b: peekZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(globalPeek, op: StdOp.condBranch(globalEmpty, thenBlockId: roundHdr.id, elseBlockId: batchStart.id))

	// ⭐ **THE BATCH, AND WHY IT CANNOT OVERFLOW THE RING.** `localPop` answered 0, so this ring was empty;
	// its only pusher is this M, which is here; a thief can only make it emptier. So the free space is the
	// whole 256 slots and the batch takes at most `GlobalBatchMax` (128) — an invariant, not a hope, and the
	// reason there is no capacity test in the loop below.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: batchStart, ids: ids)
	let batchBase = emitLoadField(module, block: batchStart, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	let batchFirst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(batchStart, op: StdOp.call(batchFirst, callee: GtDequeueName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let batchStartZero = emitConst(module, block: batchStart, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let batchNone = emitCmp(module, block: batchStart, a: batchFirst, b: batchStartZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let batchCount = ids.mint()
	batchHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(batchCount, argType: StdType.i64))
	let published = ids.mint()
	batchPublish.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(published, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(batchStart, op: StdOp.condBranch(batchNone, thenBlockId: batchPublish.id, elseBlockId: batchHdr.id))
	batchStart.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(batchPublish.id, argIds: argsOf(batchStartZero)))
	batchStart.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(batchHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(batchStartZero)))

	// The cap is one short of `GlobalBatchMax` because the first thread was taken above and is the answer.
	let batchCap = emitConst(module, block: batchHdr, value: GlobalBatchMax - 1, ids: ids)
	let batchFull = emitCmp(module, block: batchHdr, a: batchCount, b: batchCap, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(batchHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(batchFull, thenBlockId: batchPublish.id, elseBlockId: batchTake.id))
	batchHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(batchPublish.id, argIds: argsOf(batchCount)))

	let batchGt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(batchTake, op: StdOp.call(batchGt, callee: GtDequeueName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let batchTakeZero = emitConst(module, block: batchTake, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let batchDrained = emitCmp(module, block: batchTake, a: batchGt, b: batchTakeZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	var batchStore = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatchstore")
	module.setTerminator(batchTake, op: StdOp.condBranch(batchDrained, thenBlockId: batchPublish.id, elseBlockId: batchStore.id))
	batchTake.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(batchPublish.id, argIds: argsOf(batchCount)))

	let batchSlot = emitBin(module, block: batchStore, a: batchBase, b: batchCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: batchStore, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: batchStore, p: p, counter: batchSlot, ids: ids), offset: 0, value: batchGt)
	let batchStep = emitConst(module, block: batchStore, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextBatchCount = emitBin(module, block: batchStore, a: batchCount, b: batchStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(batchStore, op: StdOp.branch(batchHdr.id))
	batchStore.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(batchHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextBatchCount)))

	let publishZero = emitConst(module, block: batchPublish, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let nothingQueued = emitCmp(module, block: batchPublish, a: published, b: publishZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(batchPublish, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingQueued, thenBlockId: batchUnlock.id, elseBlockId: batchTail.id))

	emitRunqPublishTail(module, block: batchTail, p: p, delta: published, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(batchTail, op: StdOp.branch(batchUnlock.id))

	// The release is inside the function rather than at each exit for `emitGtIoCancelArm`'s reason: several
	// arms reach it, and one release block cannot have an arm added later that forgets it.
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: batchUnlock, ids: ids)
	let unlockZero = emitConst(module, block: batchUnlock, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let batchFailed = emitCmp(module, block: batchUnlock, a: batchFirst, b: unlockZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(batchUnlock, op: StdOp.condBranch(batchFailed, thenBlockId: roundHdr.id, elseBlockId: candidate.id))
	batchUnlock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(candidate.id, argIds: argsOf(batchFirst)))

	// The stealing rounds. `roundHdr` dominates every block below it, so the round index and the random start
	// are in scope throughout without being threaded through a block argument.
	let numProcs = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: globalPeek, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, ids: ids)
	let roundIndex = ids.mint()
	roundHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(roundIndex, argType: StdType.i64))
	let roundZero = emitConst(module, block: globalPeek, value: 0, ids: ids)
	globalPeek.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(roundHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(roundZero)))
	batchUnlock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(roundHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(roundZero)))

	let roundCap = emitConst(module, block: roundHdr, value: StealRounds, ids: ids)
	let roundsDone = emitCmp(module, block: roundHdr, a: roundIndex, b: roundCap, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	let giveUp = emitConst(module, block: roundHdr, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let victimStart = emitUnsignedMod(module, block: roundHdr, a: emitXorshiftStep(module, block: roundHdr, p: p, ids: ids), b: numProcs, ids: ids)
	let victimIndex = ids.mint()
	victimHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(victimIndex, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(roundHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(roundsDone, thenBlockId: candidate.id, elseBlockId: victimHdr.id))
	roundHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(candidate.id, argIds: argsOf(giveUp)))
	roundHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(victimHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(giveUp)))

	let victimsDone = emitCmp(module, block: victimHdr, a: victimIndex, b: numProcs, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(victimHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(victimsDone, thenBlockId: roundNext.id, elseBlockId: victimTest.id))

	let victimSlot = emitUnsignedMod(module, block: victimTest, a: emitBin(module, block: victimTest, a: victimStart, b: victimIndex, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids), b: numProcs, ids: ids)
	let victim = emitLoadProc(module, block: victimTest, index: victimSlot, ids: ids)
	let isSelf = emitCmp(module, block: victimTest, a: victim, b: p, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(victimTest, op: StdOp.condBranch(isSelf, thenBlockId: victimNext.id, elseBlockId: victimActive.id))

	// A P no M has claimed has an empty ring by construction, and reading it would be a load for nothing.
	let victimStatus = emitLoadField(module, block: victimActive, record: victim, offset: POffStatus, ids: ids)
	let activeConst = emitConst(module, block: victimActive, value: PStatusActive, ids: ids)
	let victimIdle = emitCmp(module, block: victimActive, a: victimStatus, b: activeConst, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(victimActive, op: StdOp.condBranch(victimIdle, thenBlockId: victimNext.id, elseBlockId: victimTry.id))

	let stolenGt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(victimTry, op: StdOp.call(stolenGt, callee: SchedStealName, args: twoArgs(p, second: victim)))
	let stealZero = emitConst(module, block: victimTry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let stealFailed = emitCmp(module, block: victimTry, a: stolenGt, b: stealZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(victimTry, op: StdOp.condBranch(stealFailed, thenBlockId: victimNext.id, elseBlockId: stole.id))

	// The counter `__Builtins.schedStealCount()` sums. Owner-written, so a plain read-modify-write.
	let stealStep = emitConst(module, block: stole, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let stolenSoFar = emitBin(module, block: stole, a: emitLoadField(module, block: stole, record: p, offset: POffStealCount, ids: ids), b: stealStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: stole, record: p, offset: POffStealCount, value: stolenSoFar)
	module.setTerminator(stole, op: StdOp.branch(candidate.id))
	stole.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(candidate.id, argIds: argsOf(stolenGt)))

	let victimStep = emitConst(module, block: victimNext, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextVictim = emitBin(module, block: victimNext, a: victimIndex, b: victimStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(victimNext, op: StdOp.branch(victimHdr.id))
	victimNext.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(victimHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextVictim)))

	let roundStep = emitConst(module, block: roundNext, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextRound = emitBin(module, block: roundNext, a: roundIndex, b: roundStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(roundNext, op: StdOp.branch(roundHdr.id))
	roundNext.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(roundHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextRound)))

	// The candidate/tombstone/hand-back tail, which `GtRuntime.__gt_coro_next` is the other caller of — one
	// body, so a change to what "dropped" means cannot reach one consumer and miss the other. ⚠ `localPop`
	// rather than `entry` is the retry, so a reclaimed thread costs no second fairness tick; that is the only
	// argument this emitter takes, and the only thing the two consumers differ in.
	emitGtTombstoneHandback(module, func: func, candidate: candidate, found: found, retry: localPop, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedFindRunnable'

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_sweep_dropped`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_sweep_dropped()` — RECLAIM THE DROPPED THREADS SITTING AT THE FRONT OF THIS P's RING AND OF
// THE GLOBAL QUEUE.** Its one caller is `__gt_promise_drop`, which is what puts one there.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT IS WHAT KEEPS A NEVER-SCHEDULED PROGRAM'S MEMORY BOUNDED, and without it the mark-and-skip
// protocol would be a leak with a spec that still passed.** `__gt_promise_drop` hands back the half a
// dropped thread's promise owns — its managed arguments and its slot in `__gt_live_count` — but its STACK
// and STRUCT can only be freed by a party that is allowed to take it out of a queue, and in a ring that is
// only the popper. A program that spawns and drops in a loop and never awaits anything (which is
// `async-promise-drop.spawn-drop-loop-bounded`, exactly) schedules NOTHING, so nothing would ever pop them.
// Sweeping the FRONT at the drop reclaims them in that shape immediately: the thread just marked is normally
// the newest AND the only one, so the ring is empty again by the time the loop comes round.
//
// ⚠ **THE FRONT ONLY, AND THE BOUND IS WHAT MAKES THAT RIGHT.** It stops at the first live thread rather
// than scanning, so the work is O(threads actually reclaimed) and each thread is reclaimed at most once ever
// — a drop can never be more than amortised O(1). A dropped thread sitting BEHIND a live one is left for
// `__sched_find_runnable` to refuse and reclaim, or for the ring OVERFLOW to reclaim in passing, and those
// two are what bound the pathological shape (a live thread pinned at the head while a loop drops behind it).
//
// ⛔ **THE PEEK-THEN-CAS IS NOT `__sched_runq_get`, AND MUST NOT BECOME IT.** `runqget` pops whatever is at
// the head; this must pop ONLY a dropped thread, because popping a live one would consume a thread nobody
// asked it to schedule. The peek is safe against the CAS window for a reason worth stating: slot `h` is
// rewritten only by a push at tail counter `h + 256k`, which requires the head to have already passed `h` —
// so a WON CAS on `head: h -> h+1` proves the slot still holds the thread that was peeked.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THAT ARGUMENT COVERS THE ACTION AND NOT THE READ, AND THE READ IS THE ONE PLACE IN THIS FILE THAT
// DEREFERENCES A THREAD IT HAS NOT YET CLAIMED.** Every other slot read here discards a lost race without
// touching what it read (`__sched_runq_get` uses `g` only after winning; `__sched_steal` copies pointers
// without following them); this one loads `GtOffTeardown` and `GtOffWaiter` THROUGH the peeked pointer
// first, because it may only claim a thread it has already identified as a tombstone. Both words are in the
// same 256-byte struct and therefore the same granule, so the two loads share one exposure, not two. Between the head load and that load a thief can
// take the thread, run it to completion, and its awaiter can hand the struct back to the slab — so the load
// can land on a freed slot. It cannot produce a wrong ACTION (a stale word that happens to read the consumer
// ticket alone loses the CAS, because the head has moved) and, while the slab keeps its granules committed,
// it cannot fault either.
// ⛔ **BUT S6's SCAVENGER DECOMMITS** (`SlabArena.__slab_arena_scavenge` → `osDecommitPages`), so a
// concurrent `__Builtins.scavengeMemory()` that drops the granule between the two loads turns this into an
// access violation. The window is a handful of instructions and needs a same-instant explicit scavenge, so
// it has never been observed — it is written down because the invariant that makes it rare is the
// ALLOCATOR'S, not this function's, and nothing here would notice the day that changes.
function buildSchedSweepDropped(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedSweepDroppedName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var ringHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"swringhdr")
	var ringPeek = module.addBlock(func, label: b"swringpeek")
	var ringTake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"swringtake")
	var ringFree = module.addBlock(func, label: b"swringfree")
	var globalStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"swglobalstart")
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"swdone")

	let p = emitLoadCurrentP(module, block: entry, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(ringHdr.id))

	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: ringHdr, record: p, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let tail = emitLoadField(module, block: ringHdr, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	let ringEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: ringHdr, a: head, b: tail, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(ringHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(ringEmpty, thenBlockId: globalStart.id, elseBlockId: ringPeek.id))

	let peeked = emitLoadField(module, block: ringPeek, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: ringPeek, p: p, counter: head, ids: ids), offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let peekedDropped = emitGtIsDropped(module, block: ringPeek, gt: peeked, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(ringPeek, op: StdOp.condBranch(peekedDropped, thenBlockId: ringTake.id, elseBlockId: globalStart.id))

	let headAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: ringTake, record: p, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let ringStep = emitConst(module, block: ringTake, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextHead = emitBin(module, block: ringTake, a: head, b: ringStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let claimed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(ringTake, op: StdOp.atomicCas(claimed, addrId: headAddr, expectedId: head, replacementId: nextHead))
	let lostRing = emitCasLost(module, block: ringTake, result: claimed, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(ringTake, op: StdOp.condBranch(lostRing, thenBlockId: ringHdr.id, elseBlockId: ringFree.id))

	module.appendOp(ringFree, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtRunnerDoneName, args: argsOf(peeked)))
	module.setTerminator(ringFree, op: StdOp.branch(ringHdr.id))

	// The global half — the SAME walk `GtRuntime.__gt_coro_sweep_dropped` is, off the two `.data` ends
	// instead of an owner's two fields. Under the lock the head cannot move, so the peek and the dequeue
	// name one thread and no compare-and-swap is needed; that argument, the front-only bound and the reason
	// the sweep exists at all live once, at `emitGtFifoSweepDropped`.
	//
	// ⚠ **NO LOCK HERE — the walk takes and releases its own, per iteration (SV1).** It has to: it calls
	// `__gt_runner_done`, which must never run under `__sched_lock`. A bracket held by this caller would make
	// the walk's release a recursive decrement that released nothing, which is exactly the latent hang
	// `GtRuntime.GtRunnerDoneName` records.
	emitGtFifoSweepDropped(module, func: func, cur: globalStart, cont: doneBlock, ends: GtQueueEnds.globalLabels, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedSweepDropped'

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_steal_count`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_steal_count() -> i64` — THE SUM OF EVERY P's STEAL COUNTER**, and the floor of
// `__Builtins.schedStealCount()`. It is the only way a Maxon program can observe that work stealing ever
// happened, which is what makes `track0/steal-torture.maxon`'s `steals > 0` an assertion rather than a hope.
//
// ⚠ **IT SUMS RATHER THAN READING A GLOBAL, AND THAT IS THE `.data` RULE THIS RUNG IS ARRANGED AROUND.** A
// `__sched_steal_count` word would appear in the `data {` section of every green-thread program's golden, so
// counting is per-P (`POffStealCount`) and the summing happens here, once, in a function no program that
// does not ask for it carries (`DeadFunctionElimination`).
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT AN ATOMIC READ AND DOES NOT NEED TO BE.** Each counter is one aligned word written by its
// own P, so every value the walk reads is a value some P really wrote; what a concurrent steal costs is
// FRESHNESS, not a torn number. A harness reads it after its work has finished, which is when it is exact.
function buildSchedStealCount(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedStealCountName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"schdr")
	var loopBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scbody")
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scdone")

	let numProcs = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let index = ids.mint()
	let total = ids.mint()
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(index, argType: StdType.i64))
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(total, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(zero, second: zero)))

	// `__sched_num_procs` is 0 until `__sched_init_procs` has run, which answers 0 for a program that asks
	// before its first `async` — the truthful answer, and the reason the walk needs no separate null test on
	// `__sched_procs`.
	let walked = emitCmp(module, block: loopHdr, a: index, b: numProcs, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(walked, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: loopBody.id))

	let stolenHere = emitLoadField(module, block: loopBody, record: emitLoadProc(module, block: loopBody, index: index, ids: ids), offset: POffStealCount, ids: ids)
	let runningTotal = emitBin(module, block: loopBody, a: total, b: stolenHere, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let step = emitConst(module, block: loopBody, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextIndex = emitBin(module, block: loopBody, a: index, b: step, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopBody, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	loopBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(nextIndex, second: runningTotal)))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.ret(total))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedStealCount'

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_worker_loop`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_worker_loop(p)` — A WORKER M's WHOLE LIFE.** A `CreateThread` callback, so `p` arrives as
// the single `lpParameter` — which is why `osThreadCreate` had to grow an argument operand: every M runs
// THIS function and is told which P it owns through exactly this word, before it has any TLS to read one
// from.
//
// It adopts `p`, then loops: drain the deferred slots, check for shutdown, dequeue a ready green thread and
// context-switch into it — coming back here when that thread yields or completes — or, with nothing to run,
// PARK on this P's wake event. On shutdown it drops the live-worker count, releases the P and returns 0.
//
// Ports v1's `emitX64SchedWorkerLoop` (`X64Backend.maxon:9205`), written in builder-built Std rather than
// bytes. ⚠ **IT NO LONGER "DEQUEUES" ANYTHING (W212)** — it asks `__sched_find_runnable`, which consults
// this P's ring, the global queue and every other P's ring in Go's order. The one thing v1 has and this
// still does not is `runnext`, and `POffRunnext` carries the reason.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A `__`-PREFIXED RUNTIME FUNCTION, so `skipsGreenThreadStackGuard` keeps the prologue stack guard
// off it — which is correct and load-bearing: it runs on a real OS-thread stack with megabytes to spare, and
// a guard here would consult a `currentGt` that does not exist until three instructions later.**
function buildSchedWorkerLoop(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedWorkerLoopName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var loopTop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wloop")
	var haveWork = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wrun")
	var handOff = module.addBlock(func, label: b"whandoff")
	var park = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wpark")
	var reDequeued = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wredeq")
	var reallyPark = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wreallypark")
	var timerBound = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wtimerbound")
	var idleBound = module.addBlock(func, label: b"widlebound")
	var doWait = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wdowait")
	var exitBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wexit")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let p = 0 as ValueId

	// Adopt: this OS thread now owns `p`. `POffStatus` is ALREADY `PStatusActive` — the spawner's CAS set it
	// before `CreateThread`, which is what makes the claim exclusive (see `emitSchedClaimProc`) — and
	// `emitSchedAdoptProc` re-stores it rather than special-casing, because a store of the value already
	// there is free and a second spelling of "which value means claimed" is not.
	emitSchedAdoptProc(module, block: entry, p: p, ids: ids)

	// ⭐ The live-worker count and its high-water mark. The count is an ATOMIC add: two Ms can enter and
	// leave concurrently, and a lost update here would make the count drift for the process's life. The MARK
	// is a plain load/compare/store, and that is deliberate — it is monotone, so a lost sample only ever
	// loses a transient reading while the mark still converges to the true peak and can never UNDER-report
	// two live workers, because this M's own increment is already visible in the reload. The bootstrap draws
	// the line in exactly the same place and says so.
	let activeAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(activeAddr, label: SchedActiveWorkersLabel))
	let one = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let priorActive = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.atomicRmw(priorActive, addrId: activeAddr, operandId: one, opcode: StdAtomicRmwOpcode.add))
	let nowActive = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: priorActive, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitSchedRaiseHighWater(module, func: func, cur: entry, live: nowActive, cont: loopTop, ids: ids)

	// loopTop: drain the deferred slots, then look for work.
	let drainResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopTop, op: StdOp.call(drainResult, callee: SchedDrainPendingName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let shutdown = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: loopTop, label: SchedShutdownFlagLabel, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: loopTop, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let stopping = emitCmp(module, block: loopTop, a: shutdown, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	var dequeue = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wdequeue")
	module.setTerminator(loopTop, op: StdOp.condBranch(stopping, thenBlockId: exitBlock.id, elseBlockId: dequeue.id))

	let g = emitSchedFindRunnable(module, block: dequeue, ids: ids)
	let dequeueZero = emitConst(module, block: dequeue, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let empty = emitCmp(module, block: dequeue, a: g, b: dequeueZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	// `haveWork` takes the dequeued GT as a block arg, because the park path re-dequeues and reaches the same
	// block with a different value.
	let runGt = ids.mint()
	haveWork.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(runGt, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(dequeue, op: StdOp.condBranch(empty, thenBlockId: park.id, elseBlockId: haveWork.id))
	dequeue.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(haveWork.id, argIds: argsOf(g)))

	// Run it, on this M's own scheduler context. The GT's waiter is this P's inline scheduler GT, so when it
	// completes or parks the trampoline switches straight back to the block below the switch.
	let mainThread = emitFieldAddr(module, block: haveWork, record: p, offset: POffMainThread, ids: ids)
	emitGtRunOne(module, run: haveWork, handOff: handOff, g: runGt, selfGt: mainThread, p: p, continuation: loopTop, ids: ids)

	// ⭐⭐ **THE PARK — A DEKKER PAIR, AND THE TWO HALVES MUST BE READ TOGETHER.** This M has just seen an
	// empty queue. An enqueuer running concurrently publishes its GT and THEN reads `idleFlag`; this side
	// publishes `idleFlag = 1` and THEN re-dequeues. Whichever order the two interleave, at least one sees
	// the other: either the enqueuer reads 1 and signals, or this side's re-dequeue observes the published
	// GT. Without the barrier between the two, x86's StoreLoad reordering lets BOTH miss — the enqueuer
	// reads a stale 0 and does not signal, this M reads a queue whose publish is not yet visible and parks
	// with work queued.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PUBLISH IS AN ATOMIC CAS, WHERE THE REFERENCE USES `XCHG`, AND THE TWO ARE THE SAME
	// INSTRUCTION FOR THIS PURPOSE.** What is needed is a LOCKED read-modify-write of the flag, because the
	// LOCK prefix is the StoreLoad fence; `lock cmpxchg` carries it exactly as `xchg`-with-memory does, and
	// carries it whether the compare succeeds or fails. The success boolean is deliberately UNREAD: the flag
	// is 1 afterwards either way, since 1 is the only other value it can hold and this M is its only writer
	// of 1. Spelling it as a CAS rather than adding an `xchg` opcode to `StdAtomicRmwOpcode` keeps the Std
	// vocabulary to what a consumer needs — the rule that band's own header states.
	let idleAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: park, record: p, offset: POffIdleFlag, ids: ids)
	let idleExpected = emitConst(module, block: park, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let idleSet = emitConst(module, block: park, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let publishedIdle = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(park, op: StdOp.atomicCas(publishedIdle, addrId: idleAddr, expectedId: idleExpected, replacementId: idleSet))

	// The RE-SEARCH that closes the window. Work found here is run at once, with the flag cleared first.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE WHOLE `__sched_find_runnable`, INCLUDING THE STEALING ROUNDS, AND NOT A QUEUE PEEK** —
	// which is what makes an M park only when there is no work ANYWHERE, rather than only when its own ring
	// and the global queue are empty. It is also the second half of the Dekker pair: whatever the enqueuer
	// published before reading `idleFlag`, this search runs after `idleFlag` was written.
	let g2 = emitSchedFindRunnable(module, block: park, ids: ids)
	let parkZero = emitConst(module, block: park, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let stillEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: park, a: g2, b: parkZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(park, op: StdOp.condBranch(stillEmpty, thenBlockId: reallyPark.id, elseBlockId: reDequeued.id))

	emitSchedClearIdle(module, block: reDequeued, p: p, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(reDequeued, op: StdOp.branch(haveWork.id))
	reDequeued.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(haveWork.id, argIds: argsOf(g2)))

	// ⭐ **THE SECOND SHUTDOWN READ IS NOT A DUPLICATE OF THE FIRST.** Between the loop top's read and this
	// point the flag may have been set and this P's event already signalled-and-consumed by the wake; a
	// blocking wait entered after that would never be woken again. Reading here — after `idleFlag` is
	// published, so a setter that arrives later is guaranteed to signal us — is what makes the wait bounded
	// by a real event rather than only by its timeout.
	let shutdownAgain = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: reallyPark, label: SchedShutdownFlagLabel, ids: ids)
	let reallyZero = emitConst(module, block: reallyPark, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let stoppingAgain = emitCmp(module, block: reallyPark, a: shutdownAgain, b: reallyZero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(reallyPark, op: StdOp.condBranch(stoppingAgain, thenBlockId: exitBlock.id, elseBlockId: timerBound.id))

	// The adaptive timeout — 2 ms while a sleep timer is pending, else 100 ms. See `ParkTimeoutWithTimersMs`.
	let timerCount = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: timerBound, label: GtTimerCountLabel, ids: ids)
	let timerZero = emitConst(module, block: timerBound, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let noTimers = emitCmp(module, block: timerBound, a: timerCount, b: timerZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let timeoutMs = ids.mint()
	doWait.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(timeoutMs, argType: StdType.i64))
	let promptMs = emitConst(module, block: timerBound, value: ParkTimeoutWithTimersMs, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(timerBound, op: StdOp.condBranch(noTimers, thenBlockId: idleBound.id, elseBlockId: doWait.id))
	timerBound.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(doWait.id, argIds: argsOf(promptMs)))
	let idleMs = emitConst(module, block: idleBound, value: ParkTimeoutIdleMs, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(idleBound, op: StdOp.branch(doWait.id))
	idleBound.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(doWait.id, argIds: argsOf(idleMs)))

	let wakeEvent = emitLoadField(module, block: doWait, record: p, offset: POffWakeEvent, ids: ids)
	let waitResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doWait, op: StdOp.osWaitHandle(waitResult, handleId: wakeEvent, timeoutMsId: timeoutMs))
	emitSchedClearIdle(module, block: doWait, p: p, ids: ids)
	// ⭐⭐ **A WORKER FIRES THE TIMERS AND REAPS THE CHILDREN TOO (SV1), AND UNTIL THIS RUNG NOTHING ON A
	// WORKER EVER DID.** The two checks are polls, not blocks — the blocking waits belong to the netpoll in
	// `__gt_drive_until`, which only a DRIVER reaches — but somebody has to run them, and before this rung the
	// only somebodies were P0's netpoll and `__gt_resched`'s poll arm. So a green thread that slept on a
	// worker M while `main` was busy in its own code waited for P0 to come back, however long that took.
	// `ParkTimeoutWithTimersMs` — 2 ms while a timer is pending — exists for exactly this and has had no
	// consumer since it was written: it is what bounds the latency of the poll below.
	//
	// ⭐ **MEASURED 2026-08-27, and the number is here because the instrument is not committed.** A green
	// thread whose `sleep(1)` runs on a worker while `main` burns 400 ms in ORDINARY USER CODE — not
	// yielding, not awaiting, so nothing on main's side polls — measures its own wait as **6-11 ms** with
	// these two calls and **400 ms** without them, 3 runs at each of `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` 2, 7 and 12.
	// ⚠ **IT IS A LATENCY EFFECT AND NOT A HANG**, which is worth saying precisely because the obvious
	// claim is the stronger one: main's own netpoll and `__gt_drain_at_exit` both poll the timer store, so
	// the deadline is noticed at the latest when main next enters a driver. What this removes is the
	// unbounded-looking gap in between, which is as long as the user code happens to run.
	let workerNow = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doWait, op: StdOp.call(workerNow, callee: GtNowNsName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	module.appendOp(doWait, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtTimerCheckName, args: argsOf(workerNow)))
	module.appendOp(doWait, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtProcCheckName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	module.setTerminator(doWait, op: StdOp.branch(loopTop.id))

	// ⭐⭐ **EXIT CLEARS BOTH `status` AND `idleFlag`, AND THE SECOND ONE IS THE ONE THAT IS EASY TO FORGET.**
	// A worker leaving through the shutdown re-check above has `idleFlag == 1` PUBLISHED. Left set, it would
	// mislead a later enqueue's idle scan into clearing the flag and signalling a DEAD P's event — a wakeup
	// delivered to nobody, and the GT that prompted it left unrun until some other M happened to look.
	// v1 records exactly this and fixes it the same way.
	// `-1` is built as `0 - 1` because `emitConst` takes a `ParsedInt`, which has no negative literal — the
	// same reason every other negative immediate in this runtime is a subtraction from zero.
	let exitZero = emitConst(module, block: exitBlock, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let exitOne = emitConst(module, block: exitBlock, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let negOne = emitBin(module, block: exitBlock, a: exitZero, b: exitOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let exitActiveAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(exitBlock, op: StdOp.globalAddr(exitActiveAddr, label: SchedActiveWorkersLabel))
	let priorOnExit = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(exitBlock, op: StdOp.atomicRmw(priorOnExit, addrId: exitActiveAddr, operandId: negOne, opcode: StdAtomicRmwOpcode.add))
	let unused = emitConst(module, block: exitBlock, value: PStatusUnused, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: exitBlock, record: p, offset: POffStatus, value: unused)
	emitSchedClearIdle(module, block: exitBlock, p: p, ids: ids)
	// A `CreateThread` callback returns a DWORD nobody reads; 0 is the conventional success.
	let exitCode = emitConst(module, block: exitBlock, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(exitBlock, op: StdOp.ret(exitCode))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedWorkerLoop'

// `p.idleFlag = 0` — the three paths that leave a park all clear it, so it is spelled once.
function emitSchedClearIdle(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let clear = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffIdleFlag, value: clear)
end 'emitSchedClearIdle'

// Raise `__sched_max_active_workers` to `live` if `live` is higher, then continue at `cont`. See the entry
// comment in `buildSchedWorkerLoop` for why this is deliberately not atomic.
function emitSchedRaiseHighWater(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, live ValueId, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var raise = module.addBlock(func, label: b"whiwater")

	let mark = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: cur, label: SchedMaxActiveWorkersLabel, ids: ids)
	let higher = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: live, b: mark, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(higher, thenBlockId: raise.id, elseBlockId: cont.id))
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: raise, label: SchedMaxActiveWorkersLabel, value: live, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(raise, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitSchedRaiseHighWater'

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_wake_or_spawn`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_wake_or_spawn()` — THE ENQUEUER'S HALF OF THE DEKKER PAIR.** Called once at the tail of
// every GREEN-THREAD publish, with the run-queue lock ALREADY RELEASED: the GT is published, and this
// decides who runs it. First scan `P[1..numProcs)` for an M that has published `idleFlag == 1` and signal
// it; if none is idle, scan again for a P no M has claimed and start one on it.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT IS UNREACHED UNTIL `spawn`, AND IT IS THE FUNCTION THAT MAKES "NO WORKER M EXISTS" TRUE RATHER
// THAN LIKELY (EC10).** It is the ONLY thing in the runtime that creates an OS thread, and its two callers
// are the green-thread arms of the publish doors and `emitSchedWakeIfOwed` — none of which any COROUTINE
// selects, because `async` publishes to its owner's queue with no wake at all. So no worker M is started at
// ANY `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` for a coroutine-only program, and `maxon-shv2/track0/pin-matrix.sh` reads that off
// the runtime's own counters: `workers=1` at 1, 2, 7 and 12. ⚠ **THAT READING IS ABOUT THE PROGRAM, NOT
// ABOUT THIS FUNCTION, AND SV1 IS WHERE THE DIFFERENCE STARTED TO MATTER.** MEASURED on this tree with
// `__gt_spawn` temporarily stamping every `async` frame as a GREEN THREAD: `steal-torture` reads
// `workers/steals` of 1/0, 2/52, 7/3970 and 7/3985 at N=1/2/7/12 with a byte-identical aggregate — this
// function creating and waking real worker Ms, which is what it was built for.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE ORDER — PUBLISH, THEN FENCE, THEN READ `idleFlag` — IS THE WHOLE CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT, the fence
// is not written here, and SINCE W212 IT IS A DIFFERENT INSTRUCTION ON EACH ARM.** Both arms have one:
//   • **THE GLOBAL TAIL** — the fence is the LOCK RELEASE the caller has just performed.
//     `LeaveCriticalSection` is a full barrier, so the queue publish inside the lock cannot be reordered past
//     the `idleFlag` reads below. That is why this function is called AFTER the unlock rather than inside the
//     critical section, and why moving it inside would be a silent correctness regression, not a scope change.
//   • **THE LOCAL RING** — there is NO lock and therefore no release to be the fence, so the ring
//     tail's own publish carries it: `emitRunqPublishTail`'s `atomicRmw add` is a `lock xadd`, and the LOCK
//     prefix is the StoreLoad barrier this scan needs. That function states it at the instruction and forbids
//     the "optimisation" back to a plain store; this is the reader it protects.
// ⚠ A caller that already HELD the lock and pushed to the ring inside it has neither fence: its nested leave
// releases nothing (`osLockEnter` is a recursive CRITICAL_SECTION). The `lock xadd` is what covers it, which
// is why `__gt_timer_check` and `__gt_proc_check` may publish inside their walk and wake once after it.
//
// ⚠ **AT `DefaultMaxProcs == 1` BOTH SCANS WOULD BE EMPTY ANYWAY** — `numProcs` is 1 and both start at
// index 1 — so even a call would cost one load and one compare. Since EC10 that is the WEAKER of the two
// reasons no worker is created; the stronger one is above, and it does not depend on a constant.
function buildSchedWakeOrSpawn(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedWakeOrSpawnName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var wakeHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wakescan")
	var wakeTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"waketest")
	var wakeIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wakeclaim")
	var wakeSignal = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wakesignal")
	var wakeNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wakenext")
	var spawnHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawnscan")
	var spawnTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawntest")
	var spawnIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawnstart")
	var spawnFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawnfailed")
	var spawnNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawnnext")
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	// A shutdown in progress must start nothing and wake nobody: a worker signalled now would find the flag
	// set and exit, and a worker CREATED now would have to be joined by a teardown that has already run.
	let shutdown = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedShutdownFlagLabel, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let stopping = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: shutdown, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let wakeIndex = ids.mint()
	wakeHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(wakeIndex, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(stopping, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: wakeHdr.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(wakeHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(one)))

	// ⭐ **THE SCANS START AT 1, NOT 0.** P[0] is the MAIN OS thread's, and the main thread is never parked on
	// a wake event — it drives the scheduler itself through `__gt_drive_until`. Signalling its event would
	// wake nobody, and claiming its P would hand a worker M the processor the main thread is already running
	// on. Both references start at 1 for the same reason.
	let wakeBound = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: wakeHdr, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, ids: ids)
	let wakeDone = emitCmp(module, block: wakeHdr, a: wakeIndex, b: wakeBound, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	let spawnIndex = ids.mint()
	spawnHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(spawnIndex, argType: StdType.i64))
	let wakeHdrOne = emitConst(module, block: wakeHdr, value: 1, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(wakeHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(wakeDone, thenBlockId: spawnHdr.id, elseBlockId: wakeTest.id))
	wakeHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(spawnHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(wakeHdrOne)))

	let wakeProc = emitLoadProc(module, block: wakeTest, index: wakeIndex, ids: ids)
	let idle = emitLoadField(module, block: wakeTest, record: wakeProc, offset: POffIdleFlag, ids: ids)
	let testZero = emitConst(module, block: wakeTest, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let notIdle = emitCmp(module, block: wakeTest, a: idle, b: testZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(wakeTest, op: StdOp.condBranch(notIdle, thenBlockId: wakeNext.id, elseBlockId: wakeIt.id))

	// ⭐ **CLAIM THE FLAG WITH A CAS, THEN SIGNAL — and the CAS is what makes "claim" a true word.** A plain
	// clear would let two enqueuers both pass the `idleFlag != 0` test above and both signal this P, which on an
	// auto-reset event is a wasted `SetEvent` rather than a fault — but it also means neither of them then looks
	// FURTHER, so a second parked M keeps sleeping while a second GT sits in the queue. Taking the flag 1→0
	// atomically makes exactly one enqueuer the waker and sends the loser on to the next index.
	//
	// ⚠ The result is READ, unlike the park's publish CAS: there the flag's value afterwards is the point and
	// the boolean is redundant; here the boolean IS the decision.
	// ⚠ `wakeTest` dominates this block, so its `wakeProc` is reused — see `spawnIt`'s note.
	let wakeItProc = wakeProc
	let idleAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: wakeIt, record: wakeItProc, offset: POffIdleFlag, ids: ids)
	let idleWas = emitConst(module, block: wakeIt, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let idleNow = emitConst(module, block: wakeIt, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let claimedIdle = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(wakeIt, op: StdOp.atomicCas(claimedIdle, addrId: idleAddr, expectedId: idleWas, replacementId: idleNow))
	let lostIdle = emitCmp(module, block: wakeIt, a: claimedIdle, b: idleNow, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(wakeIt, op: StdOp.condBranch(lostIdle, thenBlockId: wakeNext.id, elseBlockId: wakeSignal.id))

	let wakeEvent = emitLoadField(module, block: wakeSignal, record: wakeProc, offset: POffWakeEvent, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(wakeSignal, op: StdOp.osSetEvent(wakeEvent))
	module.setTerminator(wakeSignal, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	let wakeStep = emitConst(module, block: wakeNext, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let wakeNextIndex = emitBin(module, block: wakeNext, a: wakeIndex, b: wakeStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(wakeNext, op: StdOp.branch(wakeHdr.id))
	wakeNext.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(wakeHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(wakeNextIndex)))

	// Nobody idle: start an M on the first unclaimed P.
	let spawnBound = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: spawnHdr, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, ids: ids)
	let spawnDone = emitCmp(module, block: spawnHdr, a: spawnIndex, b: spawnBound, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spawnHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(spawnDone, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: spawnTest.id))

	let spawnProc = emitLoadProc(module, block: spawnTest, index: spawnIndex, ids: ids)
	let claimed = emitSchedClaimProc(module, block: spawnTest, p: spawnProc, ids: ids)
	let claimZero = emitConst(module, block: spawnTest, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let lost = emitCmp(module, block: spawnTest, a: claimed, b: claimZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spawnTest, op: StdOp.condBranch(lost, thenBlockId: spawnNext.id, elseBlockId: spawnIt.id))

	// ⚠ `spawnTest` dominates this block, so `spawnProc` is in scope and is reused rather than re-derived —
	// the index→P mapping is spelled once per scan.
	let spawnItProc = spawnProc
	let entryPtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnIt, op: StdOp.funcAddr(entryPtr, funcName: SchedWorkerLoopName))
	let threadHandle = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnIt, op: StdOp.osThreadCreate(threadHandle, entryPtrId: entryPtr, argId: spawnItProc))
	emitStoreField(module, block: spawnIt, record: spawnItProc, offset: POffOsThreadHandle, value: threadHandle)
	// ⭐ **A `CreateThread` THAT FAILED MUST GIVE THE P BACK, and the claim above is what makes that
	// necessary.** The CAS already marked this P `active`, so a null handle would leave a processor that no M
	// will ever adopt and that no later spawn can claim — the machine quietly losing a core for the life of the
	// process. Nothing hangs (the main thread's driver still services the queue), which is exactly why the leak
	// would go unnoticed. Releasing it is one store, and the next enqueue's scan simply tries again.
	let spawnZero = emitConst(module, block: spawnIt, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let threadFailed = emitCmp(module, block: spawnIt, a: threadHandle, b: spawnZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spawnIt, op: StdOp.condBranch(threadFailed, thenBlockId: spawnFailed.id, elseBlockId: doneBlock.id))

	let unusedAgain = emitConst(module, block: spawnFailed, value: PStatusUnused, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: spawnFailed, record: spawnProc, offset: POffStatus, value: unusedAgain)
	module.setTerminator(spawnFailed, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	let spawnStep = emitConst(module, block: spawnNext, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let spawnNextIndex = emitBin(module, block: spawnNext, a: spawnIndex, b: spawnStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spawnNext, op: StdOp.branch(spawnHdr.id))
	spawnNext.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(spawnHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(spawnNextIndex)))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedWakeOrSpawn'

// ⭐⭐ **CLAIM `p` FOR A NEW WORKER M — `CAS(p.status, unused -> active)`, ANSWERING 1 IF THIS THREAD WON.**
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT MUST BE A CAS, AND A PLAIN TEST-AND-SET HERE IS A HEAP-CORRUPTION BUG WITH A NAME.** The whole
// wake/spawn phase runs with the run-queue lock RELEASED (see `buildSchedWakeOrSpawn`), so several Ms reach
// this scan at once. With a read/test/store, two of them observe `status == 0` and both `CreateThread` a
// worker on the SAME P — two Ms sharing one P id, hence one allocator shard, whose lockless same-P
// allocations then hand THE SAME SLOT to both threads. v1 records that failure verbatim
// (`X64Backend.maxon:8910-8919`) and both of its backends fixed it independently; the shared C# emitter
// **still has the plain test-and-set**, which is why this is the one place in this file that does not follow
// the bootstrap.
//
// The loser's answer is 0 and its caller simply moves to the next index, so exactly one M spawns.
function emitSchedClaimProc(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let statusAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffStatus, ids: ids)
	let expected = emitConst(module, block: block, value: PStatusUnused, ids: ids)
	let replacement = emitConst(module, block: block, value: PStatusActive, ids: ids)
	let won = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.atomicCas(won, addrId: statusAddr, expectedId: expected, replacementId: replacement))
	return won
end 'emitSchedClaimProc'

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_stop_workers`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__sched_stop_workers()` — SEAL THE SCHEDULER AND JOIN EVERY WORKER M OUT (SV1).** Its one caller
// is `GtRuntime.__gt_drain_at_exit`, which has already driven the queues empty; this is what makes the Ms
// that were servicing them STOP, and stop before anything downstream reads a counter they can still step.
//
// Three steps, in this order and for these reasons:
//
//   1. **`__sched_shutdown_flag = 1` FIRST**, so a worker that wakes for any reason at all — a signal, its
//      own park timeout, work it finds — reads it at the loop top or at the second read beside its park and
//      leaves. Setting it after the signals would let a worker consume the signal, find nothing, and park
//      again on a 100 ms timeout.
//   2. **`SetEvent` on EVERY P's wake event.** The events are AUTO-reset, so a signal that arrives while its
//      M is still running is not lost — it leaves the event signalled and the next `osWaitHandle` returns at
//      once. Signalling a P no M ever claimed is harmless for the same reason: nobody waits on it.
//   3. **JOIN each worker's thread handle, INFINITE.** ⛔ **The alternative is not "exit sooner", it is a
//      RACE:** a worker's `__gt_reclaim` runs releaser decrefs, which step the very allocation counters
//      `__mm_leak_check` reads two calls later on the exit path. `ExitProcess` would kill the workers
//      mid-decref and the gate would read a count that was still moving — an intermittent exit 101 on a
//      program with no leak. `POffOsThreadHandle` was stored *"for the teardown that joins the workers
//      out"*; this is that teardown, and it is why the handle was kept rather than reserved.
//
// ⚠ **P[0]'s HANDLE IS ZERO AND IS SKIPPED BY THE SAME TEST EVERY UNCLAIMED P IS.** The main OS thread was
// never created by this runtime, so there is no handle for it and joining it would be joining ourselves.
// The walk starts at 0 anyway, because the SIGNAL half is harmless there and a scan that started at 1 would
// be a second place the *"P[0] is main's"* fact is written down.
function buildSchedStopWorkers(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedStopWorkersName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var wakeHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopwakehdr")
	var wakeBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopwakebody")
	var joinStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopjoinstart")
	var joinHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopjoinhdr")
	var joinBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopjoinbody")
	var joinWait = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopjoinwait")
	var joinNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopjoinnext")
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopdone")

	let one = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 1, ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedShutdownFlagLabel, value: one, ids: ids)
	let numProcs = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let wakeIndex = ids.mint()
	wakeHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(wakeIndex, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(wakeHdr.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(wakeHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))

	let wakeDone = emitCmp(module, block: wakeHdr, a: wakeIndex, b: numProcs, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(wakeHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(wakeDone, thenBlockId: joinStart.id, elseBlockId: wakeBody.id))

	let wakeProc = emitLoadProc(module, block: wakeBody, index: wakeIndex, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(wakeBody, op: StdOp.osSetEvent(emitLoadField(module, block: wakeBody, record: wakeProc, offset: POffWakeEvent, ids: ids)))
	let wakeStep = emitConst(module, block: wakeBody, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextWake = emitBin(module, block: wakeBody, a: wakeIndex, b: wakeStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(wakeBody, op: StdOp.branch(wakeHdr.id))
	wakeBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(wakeHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextWake)))

	// The join is a SECOND walk rather than a step of the first: every worker must have been told to stop
	// before we block on any one of them, or a P signalled late would be joined while it was still parked on
	// an event nobody had set.
	let joinIndex = ids.mint()
	joinHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(joinIndex, argType: StdType.i64))
	let joinStartZero = emitConst(module, block: joinStart, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(joinStart, op: StdOp.branch(joinHdr.id))
	joinStart.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(joinHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(joinStartZero)))

	let joinDone = emitCmp(module, block: joinHdr, a: joinIndex, b: numProcs, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(joinHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(joinDone, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: joinBody.id))

	let joinProc = emitLoadProc(module, block: joinBody, index: joinIndex, ids: ids)
	let threadHandle = emitLoadField(module, block: joinBody, record: joinProc, offset: POffOsThreadHandle, ids: ids)
	let joinZero = emitConst(module, block: joinBody, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let noThread = emitCmp(module, block: joinBody, a: threadHandle, b: joinZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(joinBody, op: StdOp.condBranch(noThread, thenBlockId: joinNext.id, elseBlockId: joinWait.id))

	let infinite = emitConst(module, block: joinWait, value: WaitInfinite, ids: ids)
	let joinResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(joinWait, op: StdOp.osWaitHandle(joinResult, handleId: threadHandle, timeoutMsId: infinite))
	module.setTerminator(joinWait, op: StdOp.branch(joinNext.id))

	let joinStep = emitConst(module, block: joinNext, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextJoin = emitBin(module, block: joinNext, a: joinIndex, b: joinStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(joinNext, op: StdOp.branch(joinHdr.id))
	joinNext.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(joinHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextJoin)))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedStopWorkers'
